   UNIT 3 — EXPRESSIONS & EQUATIONS  (his Chapter 3)
   ============================================================ */
function linStr(m,b){
  if(m===0)return String(b);
  const mm=m===1?'x':m===-1?'-x':m+'x';
  if(b===0)return mm;
  return mm+(b<0?'-':'+')+Math.abs(b);
}
function linAcc(m,b){
  const out=[linStr(m,b)];
  if(m!==0&&b!==0){
    const am=Math.abs(m)===1?'x':Math.abs(m)+'x';
    out.push(b+(m<0?'-':'+')+am);
  }
  return out;
}
function linHTML(m,b){
  if(m===0)return sgn(b);
  const mm=m===1?'x':m===-1?'\u2212x':sgn(m)+'x';
  if(b===0)return mm;
  return mm+(b<0?' \u2212 ':' + ')+Math.abs(b);
}

T({unit:'Expressions & equations',short:'Simplifying expressions',
title:'Simplifying algebraic expressions',
idea:`<p>Two ideas, and they're both about <b>what things are</b>.</p>
<p><b>Like terms</b> are terms with the same variable raised to the same power. 5x and \u22122x are like terms; 5x and 5 are not; 5x and 5x<sup>2</sup> are not. You can only combine like terms, because the variable is functioning as a <b>unit</b>. 5 apples plus 3 apples is 8 apples. 5 apples plus 3 oranges is just 5 apples and 3 oranges — there's no single name for the pile.</p>
<p>And when you combine them, you add the <b>coefficients</b> only. 5x + 3x = 8x, not 8x<sup>2</sup>. You're counting how many x's you have; you are not multiplying them together.</p>
<p><b>Distributing</b> handles the case where a fence is in the way. 4(2x \u2212 5) means 4 copies of everything inside, so the 4 has to reach <b>every</b> term: 8x \u2212 20. It's the same distributive property you used on 43 \u00d7 27 back in Chapter 1 — split, multiply each piece, reassemble.</p>`,
watch:`When a minus sign sits in front of a parenthesis, it distributes too. \u2212(x \u2212 7) is \u2212x + 7, because that minus is really \u22121 multiplying <i>both</i> terms. Forgetting the second sign flip is the single most common error in this topic.`,
gen(){
  const r=Math.random();
  if(r<0.34){
    const a=ri(2,9),b=ri(2,14),c=ri(2,9),d=ri(2,14);
    const m=a-c,k=d-b;
    return{
      q:`Simplify: <b>${a}x + ${b} \u2212 ${c}x + ${d} \u2212 ${2*b}</b>`,hint:'like 3x+7',
      ans:linStr(m,d-b),accepts:A(linAcc(m,d-b)),
      steps:[
        [`Sort the terms into groups: the x-terms are ${a}x and \u2212${c}x; the plain numbers are ${b}, ${d} and \u2212${2*b}.`,
         `Each term carries the sign written in front of it — the \u2212 belongs to the ${c}x, not to the ${b} before it. Grouping first means you never accidentally combine an x-term with a number.`],
        [`Combine the x-terms: ${a}x \u2212 ${c}x = <b>${linStr(m,0)}</b>.`,
         `You had ${a} x's, you took away ${c} of them, so ${m} x's are left. Only the coefficients changed; x stayed x, because you were counting them, not multiplying them.`],
        [`Combine the numbers: ${b} + ${d} \u2212 ${2*b} = <b>${d-b}</b>.`,
         `These are all the same kind of thing — plain constants — so they pile up freely. Left to right, signs attached.`],
        [`Put the two groups back together: <b>${linHTML(m,d-b)}</b>.`,
         `And they stay separate. ${linStr(m,0)} and ${d-b} are unlike terms, so this is as simplified as it gets — there is no operation that would merge them into one term.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.67){
    const a=ri(2,8)*(Math.random()<0.3?-1:1),b=ri(2,9),c=ri(2,12);
    return{
      q:`Distribute and simplify: <b>${par(a)}(${b}x \u2212 ${c})</b>`,hint:'like 8x-20',
      ans:linStr(a*b,-a*c),accepts:A(linAcc(a*b,-a*c)),
      steps:[
        [`The ${par(a)} multiplies <b>every</b> term inside, not just the first one.`,
         `${par(a)}(${b}x \u2212 ${c}) means ${Math.abs(a)} cop${Math.abs(a)===1?'y':'ies'} of the entire quantity ${b}x \u2212 ${c}. If you only hit the ${b}x, you'd be making copies of half the thing.`],
        [`First term: ${par(a)} \u00b7 ${b}x = <b>${linStr(a*b,0)}</b>.`,
         `Multiply the coefficients and carry the x along. ${a<0?`Different signs here, so this term comes out negative.`:`Both positive, so this term stays positive.`}`],
        [`Second term: ${par(a)} \u00b7 (\u2212${c}) = <b>${sgn(-a*c)}</b>.`,
         `Bring the sign with the term. The expression is ${b}x <i>plus negative</i> ${c}, so what you're multiplying is \u2212${c}. ${(a<0)?`Negative times negative gives a positive here.`:`Positive times negative gives a negative here.`}`],
        [`Write the result: <b>${linHTML(a*b,-a*c)}</b>.`,
         `Two unlike terms, so nothing else combines. Sanity-check with x = 1: the original gives ${par(a)}(${b} \u2212 ${c}) = ${a*(b-c)}, and the answer gives ${a*b} ${-a*c<0?'\u2212 '+Math.abs(a*c):'+ '+a*c*-1*-1} = ${a*b-a*c}. \u2713`]
      ]};
  }
  const a=ri(2,6),b=ri(2,9),c=ri(2,6),d=ri(2,9);
  const m=a-c,k=a*b+c*d;
  return{
    q:`Simplify: <b>${a}(x + ${b}) \u2212 ${c}(x \u2212 ${d})</b>`,hint:'like 3x+7',
    ans:linStr(m,k),accepts:A(linAcc(m,k)),
    steps:[
      [`Distribute the ${a}: ${a}(x + ${b}) = <b>${a}x + ${a*b}</b>.`,
       `Every term inside gets multiplied. Straightforward — no sign surprises on this one.`],
      [`Now the dangerous one. The sign in front is <b>\u2212${c}</b>, so distribute \u2212${c}, not ${c}: \u2212${c}(x \u2212 ${d}) = <b>\u2212${c}x + ${c*d}</b>.`,
       `Watch the second term: \u2212${c} \u00b7 (\u2212${d}) = +${c*d}. Two negatives multiply to a positive. This sign flip is exactly what people miss, and it changes the answer by ${2*c*d}.`],
      [`Rewrite the whole thing: ${a}x + ${a*b} \u2212 ${c}x + ${c*d}`,
       `The fences are gone, so every term is now free-standing and available to be combined with its own kind.`],
      [`Combine like terms: (${a}x \u2212 ${c}x) = ${linStr(m,0)} and (${a*b} + ${c*d}) = ${k} &rarr; <b>${linHTML(m,k)}</b>.`,
       `x-terms with x-terms, constants with constants. Nothing merges across the two groups because they aren't the same kind of thing.`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- 15. one-step equations ---------- */
T({unit:'Expressions & equations',short:'One-step equations',
title:'Solving basic equations',
idea:`<p>An equation is a <b>balance scale</b>. The equals sign is the pivot, and it's a promise that both sides weigh the same. That promise is the only rule you need: <b>whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other</b>, or the scale tips and the statement becomes false.</p>
<p>Your goal is always the same: get the variable <b>alone</b> on one side. To do that you look at what's currently being done to it, and you undo it with the <b>inverse operation</b>. Addition undoes subtraction. Multiplication undoes division. They're built to cancel each other.</p>
<p>Then <b>check</b>. Put your answer back into the original equation and confirm both sides really do come out equal. This isn't busywork — it's the only step that tells you whether you were right, and it takes ten seconds.</p>`,
watch:`You're not "moving the number to the other side and flipping the sign." That's a description of what happens, not a reason. What you're actually doing is adding the opposite to <i>both</i> sides so it cancels on one. Do it that way and you'll never wonder which sign to use.`,
gen(){
  const t=ri(1,4);
  if(t===1){
    const x=ri(2,25)*(Math.random()<0.35?-1:1),a=ri(2,30),b=x+a;
    return{q:`Solve for x: &nbsp;<b>x + ${a} = ${sgn(b)}</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:sgn(x),accepts:A(String(x)),
      steps:[
        [`x currently has <b>${a} added</b> to it. The inverse of adding ${a} is subtracting ${a}.`,
         `Name the operation before you undo it. Whatever is happening to x is what you have to reverse — that's the entire strategy for every equation you'll ever solve.`],
        [`Subtract ${a} from <b>both</b> sides: &nbsp; x + ${a} \u2212 ${a} = ${sgn(b)} \u2212 ${a}`,
         `Both sides, always. Removing ${a} from only the left would break the balance and the equation would stop being true. Doing it to both keeps the scale level.`],
        [`Left side: ${a} \u2212 ${a} = 0, so x is alone. Right side: ${sgn(b)} \u2212 ${a} = <b>${sgn(x)}</b>.`,
         `That's why you chose subtraction — it was engineered to make the left side collapse to zero and leave x by itself. Zero is the additive identity: adding it changes nothing.`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${sgn(x)} + ${a} = ${sgn(b)} \u2713`,
         `Both sides match, so the scale really does balance at x = ${sgn(x)}. Never skip this — it's free proof.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(t===2){
    const x=ri(2,25)*(Math.random()<0.35?-1:1),a=ri(2,30),b=x-a;
    return{q:`Solve for x: &nbsp;<b>x \u2212 ${a} = ${sgn(b)}</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:sgn(x),accepts:A(String(x)),
      steps:[
        [`x has <b>${a} subtracted</b> from it. The inverse is adding ${a}.`,
         `Subtraction and addition are inverse operations — each one exactly undoes the other, which is what makes this work.`],
        [`Add ${a} to <b>both</b> sides: &nbsp; x \u2212 ${a} + ${a} = ${sgn(b)} + ${a}`,
         `Same balance rule. You're allowed to add anything you like to an equation as long as both sides get the identical treatment.`],
        [`Left: \u2212${a} + ${a} = 0, leaving x. Right: ${sgn(b)} + ${a} = <b>${sgn(x)}</b>.`,
         `\u2212${a} and +${a} are opposites, and opposites always add to zero. That cancellation is the whole reason you picked +${a} and not some other number.`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${sgn(x)} \u2212 ${a} = ${sgn(b)} \u2713`,
         `Substituting back is the only way to be certain. If it doesn't check, you know immediately instead of finding out on a test.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(t===3){
    const a=ri(2,12)*(Math.random()<0.3?-1:1),x=ri(2,15)*(Math.random()<0.35?-1:1),b=a*x;
    return{q:`Solve for x: &nbsp;<b>${sgn(a)}x = ${sgn(b)}</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:sgn(x),accepts:A(String(x)),
      steps:[
        [`x is being <b>multiplied by ${sgn(a)}</b>. The inverse of multiplying is dividing.`,
         `${sgn(a)}x means ${sgn(a)} times x. There's no addition anywhere in this equation, so adding or subtracting would get you nowhere — you have to undo the operation that's actually there.`],
        [`Divide <b>both</b> sides by ${sgn(a)}: &nbsp; ${fr(sgn(a)+'x',sgn(a))} = ${fr(sgn(b),sgn(a))}`,
         `Both sides again. Dividing only the left would shrink one pan of the scale and the equation would no longer hold.`],
        [`Left: ${sgn(a)} \u00f7 ${sgn(a)} = 1, and 1 \u00b7 x is just x. Right: ${sgn(b)} \u00f7 ${sgn(a)} = <b>${sgn(x)}</b>.`,
         `A number over itself is 1, and 1 is the multiplicative identity — multiplying by it changes nothing. That's how x ends up standing alone.${(a<0)!==(b<0)?` Different signs in the division, so the answer is negative.`:` Same signs in the division, so the answer is positive.`}`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${sgn(a)} \u00b7 ${par(x)} = ${sgn(b)} \u2713`,
         `Multiply back and confirm. On sign problems especially, this catches errors instantly.`]
      ]};
  }
  const a=ri(2,9),x=ri(2,15)*(Math.random()<0.35?-1:1),b=x;
  return{q:`Solve for x: &nbsp;<b>${fr('x',a)} = ${sgn(b)}</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:sgn(a*b),accepts:A(String(a*b)),
    steps:[
      [`x is being <b>divided by ${a}</b>. The inverse is multiplying by ${a}.`,
       `${fr('x',a)} is another way of writing x \u00f7 ${a}. A fraction bar is a division sign that got tired of standing up.`],
      [`Multiply <b>both</b> sides by ${a}: &nbsp; ${a} \u00b7 ${fr('x',a)} = ${a} \u00b7 ${par(b)}`,
       `Both sides. The balance rule doesn't care which operation you use — it only cares that both pans get the same treatment.`],
      [`Left: ${a} \u00f7 ${a} = 1, so you're left with x. Right: ${a} \u00b7 ${sgn(b)} = <b>${sgn(a*b)}</b>.`,
       `Multiplying by ${a} exactly undoes dividing by ${a}, because ${a} \u00b7 ${fr(1,a)} = 1. Inverse operations are defined by that cancellation.`],
      [`<b>Check:</b> ${sgn(a*b)} \u00f7 ${a} = ${sgn(b)} \u2713`,
       `It balances, so x = ${sgn(a*b)} is genuinely the solution and not just the number you ended up with.`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- 16. multi-step equations ---------- */
T({unit:'Expressions & equations',short:'General equations',
title:'Solving general equations',
idea:`<p>Now there's more than one thing being done to x, so you need an order for undoing them. Here it is: <b>undo in the reverse order of operations.</b></p>
<p>Think about getting dressed. You put on socks, then shoes. To undo it you take off shoes, then socks — reverse order. An equation like 3x + 7 = 22 did multiplication first, then addition. So you undo the <b>addition first</b>, then the multiplication.</p>
<p>The full routine, in order: <b>1.</b> simplify each side on its own (distribute, combine like terms). <b>2.</b> get all the variable terms on one side and all the numbers on the other. <b>3.</b> divide by the coefficient. <b>4.</b> check.</p>
<p>Step 2 works because of the same balance rule: subtracting 2x from both sides removes it from one side and doesn't disturb the equality.</p>`,
watch:`Undo addition and subtraction <b>before</b> multiplication and division. If you divide 3x + 7 = 22 by 3 first, you get x + ${fr(7,3)} = ${fr(22,3)} — legal, but you've just handed yourself fractions for no reason.`,
gen(){
  const t=ri(1,3);
  if(t===1){
    const a=ri(2,9),x=ri(2,14)*(Math.random()<0.3?-1:1),b=ri(2,20)*(Math.random()<0.4?-1:1),c=a*x+b;
    return{q:`Solve for x: &nbsp;<b>${a}x ${b<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b)} = ${sgn(c)}</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:sgn(x),accepts:A(String(x)),
      steps:[
        [`Two things are happening to x: it's multiplied by ${a}, then ${b<0?`${Math.abs(b)} is subtracted`:`${b} is added`}. Undo them in <b>reverse order</b>.`,
         `Order of operations built this expression by multiplying first and ${b<0?'subtracting':'adding'} second. To take it apart you run the film backwards: undo the ${b<0?'subtraction':'addition'} first, because it was the last thing applied.`],
        [`${b<0?`Add ${Math.abs(b)} to`:`Subtract ${b} from`} both sides: &nbsp; ${a}x = ${sgn(c)} ${b<0?'+':'\u2212'} ${Math.abs(b)} = <b>${sgn(a*x)}</b>`,
         `That clears the constant off the left, leaving only the ${a}x term. Both sides got the identical treatment, so the balance is intact.`],
        [`Now undo the multiplication \u2014 divide both sides by ${a}: &nbsp; x = ${fr(sgn(a*x),a)} = <b>${sgn(x)}</b>`,
         `${a}x means ${a} times x, so division is the inverse that isolates x. And notice how clean this division is: waiting until now is exactly why no fractions showed up.`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${a}(${sgn(x)}) ${b<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b)} = ${sgn(a*x)} ${b<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b)} = ${sgn(c)} \u2713`,
         `Substitute into the <i>original</i> equation, not into a line you rewrote. If you made an error along the way, only the original will expose it.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(t===2){
    let a,c,x,b,d,g=0;
    do{a=ri(3,11);c=ri(2,9);x=ri(2,12)*(Math.random()<0.3?-1:1);b=ri(2,18);d=(a-c)*x+b;}
    while(a===c&&g++<50);
    return{q:`Solve for x: &nbsp;<b>${a}x + ${b} = ${c}x + ${sgn(d)}</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:sgn(x),accepts:A(String(x)),
      steps:[
        [`Variables live on both sides. Collect them on one side \u2014 subtract ${c}x from <b>both</b> sides.`,
         `You can't isolate x while it appears in two places. Removing ${c}x from both sides is legal by the balance rule, and it makes the right side variable-free.`],
        [`${a}x \u2212 ${c}x = ${linStr(a-c,0)}, so: &nbsp; ${linStr(a-c,0)} + ${b} = ${sgn(d)}`,
         `Like terms combine by adding coefficients: ${a} x's minus ${c} x's leaves ${a-c} x's. On the right, ${c}x \u2212 ${c}x = 0 and vanishes entirely.`],
        [`Now subtract ${b} from both sides: &nbsp; ${linStr(a-c,0)} = ${sgn(d)} \u2212 ${b} = <b>${sgn((a-c)*x)}</b>`,
         `Constants to the other side, same balance rule. You're back to the familiar shape from the last topic: coefficient times x equals a number.`],
        [`Divide both sides by ${a-c}: &nbsp; x = <b>${sgn(x)}</b>`,
         `Last step, because division is the last thing to undo. Dividing earlier would have dragged fractions through every line.`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> left = ${a}(${sgn(x)}) + ${b} = ${sgn(a*x+b)}; right = ${c}(${sgn(x)}) + ${sgn(d)} = ${sgn(c*x+d)} \u2713`,
         `Both sides land on the same number, which is exactly what the equals sign was promising.`]
      ]};
  }
  const a=ri(2,7),b=ri(2,9)*(Math.random()<0.35?-1:1),x=ri(2,12)*(Math.random()<0.3?-1:1),c=a*(x+b);
  return{q:`Solve for x: &nbsp;<b>${a}(x ${b<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b)}) = ${sgn(c)}</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:sgn(x),accepts:A(String(x)),
    steps:[
      [`Clear the fence first \u2014 distribute the ${a}: &nbsp; ${a}x ${b<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(a*b)} = ${sgn(c)}`,
       `The ${a} multiplies both terms inside, not just the x. Once the parentheses are gone the equation has the standard shape you already know how to solve.`],
      [`${b<0?`Add ${Math.abs(a*b)} to`:`Subtract ${a*b} from`} both sides: &nbsp; ${a}x = <b>${sgn(a*x)}</b>`,
       `Undo the ${b<0?'subtraction':'addition'} first, since it was applied last. Reverse order of operations, every time.`],
      [`Divide both sides by ${a}: &nbsp; x = <b>${sgn(x)}</b>`,
       `Multiplication is undone last. That ordering is what keeps the arithmetic clean.`],
      [`<b>Check:</b> ${a}(${sgn(x)} ${b<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b)}) = ${a}(${sgn(x+b)}) = ${sgn(c)} \u2713`,
       `Checking in the original form also confirms you distributed correctly at the start — one check validates the whole chain.`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- 17. translating into equations ---------- */
T({unit:'Expressions & equations',short:'Word problems',
title:'Translating sentences into equations',
idea:`<p>Word problems aren't harder math. They're the same math with a translation step in front. Do the translation carefully and the algebra is stuff you already finished.</p>
<p>The routine: <b>1.</b> Decide what the unknown is and name it — write "let x = the number." <b>2.</b> Find the verb. "Is," "was," "equals," "gives" all mean <b>=</b>. That's your pivot; everything before it goes left, everything after goes right. <b>3.</b> Translate the phrases into operations. <b>4.</b> Solve. <b>5.</b> Answer the question that was actually asked.</p>
<p>The vocabulary is small: <b>sum, more than, increased by</b> \u2192 add. <b>Difference, less than, decreased by</b> \u2192 subtract. <b>Product, times, twice, of</b> \u2192 multiply. <b>Quotient, per, divided by</b> \u2192 divide.</p>`,
watch:`"Less than" reverses the order. "7 less than a number" is x \u2212 7, never 7 \u2212 x. And "the sum of a number and 4, doubled" needs parentheses: 2(x + 4). Read the punctuation — commas are usually telling you where the fence goes.`,
gen(){
  const t=ri(1,4);
  if(t===1){
    const k=ri(2,9),m=ri(3,25),x=ri(2,20),tot=k*x+m;
    return{q:`<b>${m} more than ${k} times a number is ${tot}. Find the number.</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:String(x),accepts:A(String(x)),
      steps:[
        [`Name the unknown: <b>let x = the number</b>.`,
         `Write this line down every single time. Half of word-problem confusion is losing track of what your variable stands for by line four.`],
        [`Find the verb: "<b>is</b>" means =. Everything before it is the left side, ${tot} is the right side.`,
         `The equals sign is the hinge of the sentence. Locating it splits one long confusing sentence into two short translatable pieces.`],
        [`Translate the left: "${k} times a number" = ${k}x, and "${m} more than" that adds ${m} &rarr; <b>${k}x + ${m} = ${tot}</b>`,
         `"More than" is addition. Note that ${m} + ${k}x would also be correct here — addition doesn't care about order — but writing the variable term first keeps the equation in the standard shape.`],
        [`Solve: subtract ${m} from both sides &rarr; ${k}x = ${k*x}. Divide by ${k} &rarr; <b>x = ${x}</b>.`,
         `Undo in reverse order of operations: the addition goes first, the multiplication second.`],
        [`<b>Check against the sentence:</b> ${k} times ${x} is ${k*x}, and ${m} more than that is ${tot}. \u2713`,
         `Check against the original <i>words</i>, not just your equation. That catches translation errors, which your algebra never would.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(t===2){
    const k=ri(2,9),m=ri(3,20),x=ri(4,25),res=k*x-m;
    return{q:`<b>${m} less than ${k} times a number gives ${res}. Find the number.</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:String(x),accepts:A(String(x)),
      steps:[
        [`<b>Let x = the number.</b> The verb "gives" means =.`,
         `"Gives," "is," "yields," "results in" — they all mark the equals sign. Different words, same hinge.`],
        [`"${k} times a number" = ${k}x. Then "${m} less than" that quantity: &nbsp;<b>${k}x \u2212 ${m} = ${res}</b>`,
         `Careful here. The sentence says ${m} first, but ${m} is what gets <i>removed</i> from ${k}x. Writing ${m} \u2212 ${k}x would be a different problem with a different answer, because subtraction isn't reversible.`],
        [`Add ${m} to both sides: ${k}x = ${k*x}. Divide by ${k}: <b>x = ${x}</b>.`,
         `Reverse order of operations again — clear the constant, then the coefficient.`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${k} \u00b7 ${x} = ${k*x}, and ${m} less than ${k*x} is ${res}. \u2713`,
         `Reading the check back in English confirms the translation as well as the arithmetic.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(t===3){
    const n=ri(12,60),tot=n+(n+1);
    return{q:`<b>The sum of two consecutive integers is ${tot}. Find the smaller one.</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:String(n),accepts:A(String(n)),
      steps:[
        [`Consecutive means back-to-back on the number line. <b>Let x = the smaller integer</b>; then the next one is <b>x + 1</b>.`,
         `This is the key move: define <i>one</i> variable and express everything else in terms of it. Using two letters would need two equations, and you've only got one sentence.`],
        [`"The sum ... is ${tot}" &rarr; <b>x + (x + 1) = ${tot}</b>`,
         `"Sum" means add the two quantities. The parentheses aren't strictly required here, but they keep it obvious that x + 1 is a single number.`],
        [`Combine like terms: <b>2x + 1 = ${tot}</b>`,
         `x + x is 2x — you have two of them. The constant 1 stays on its own since it isn't like the x-terms.`],
        [`Subtract 1: 2x = ${tot-1}. Divide by 2: <b>x = ${n}</b>.`,
         `Standard two-step solve, exactly like the last topic.`],
        [`<b>Answer the actual question.</b> The smaller integer is <b>${n}</b> (the other is ${n+1}, and ${n} + ${n+1} = ${tot}). \u2713`,
         `The question asked for the smaller one, so ${n} is the answer. Solving for x and answering the question are two different steps, and problems love to ask for the <i>other</i> quantity.`]
      ]};
  }
  const w=ri(4,25),L=w+ri(2,12),P=2*w+2*L,d=L-w;
  return{q:`<b>A rectangle's length is ${d} more than its width. The perimeter is ${P}. Find the width.</b>`,hint:'an integer',ans:String(w),accepts:A(String(w)),
    steps:[
      [`<b>Let x = the width.</b> Then the length is <b>x + ${d}</b>.`,
       `The length was described <i>in terms of</i> the width, so the width is the natural choice for x. Pick the quantity everything else is measured against and the rest translates itself.`],
      [`Perimeter means all four sides: <b>2x + 2(x + ${d}) = ${P}</b>`,
       `A rectangle has two widths and two lengths. Writing the formula from the meaning — rather than recalling it — is what keeps you from mixing it up with area.`],
      [`Distribute and combine: 2x + 2x + ${2*d} = ${P} &rarr; <b>4x + ${2*d} = ${P}</b>`,
       `The 2 hits both terms inside. Then the x-terms combine: 2x + 2x = 4x, which makes sense — four sides, and each one contributes an x.`],
      [`Subtract ${2*d}: 4x = ${P-2*d}. Divide by 4: <b>x = ${w}</b>.`,
       `Reverse order of operations to isolate x.`],
      [`<b>Check:</b> width ${w}, length ${L}, perimeter = 2(${w}) + 2(${L}) = ${P}. \u2713`,
       `Both conditions from the problem hold: the length really is ${d} more than the width, and the perimeter really is ${P}. A checked answer satisfies <i>every</i> sentence in the problem, not just the last one.`]
    ]};
}});

/* ============================================================
   UNIT 4 — FRACTIONS  (his Chapter 4)
   ============================================================ */
function pfAcc(n){
  const f=pfac(n),m={};f.forEach(p=>m[p]=(m[p]||0)+1);
  const keys=Object.keys(m).map(Number).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const flat=f.slice().sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const ex=keys.map(p=>m[p]>1?p+'^'+m[p]:String(p));
  return A(flat.join('*'),flat.join('x'),ex.join('*'),ex.join('x'));
}

T({unit:'Fractions',short:'What a fraction is',
title:'Introduction to fractions',
idea:`<p>A fraction is a <b>division problem that hasn't been carried out yet</b>. ${fr(3,4)} literally means 3 \u00f7 4.</p>
<p>Read it in two parts. The <b>denominator</b> (bottom) tells you <b>how many equal pieces the whole was cut into</b> — it names the <i>size</i> of the piece. The <b>numerator</b> (top) tells you <b>how many of those pieces you have</b> — it <i>counts</i> them. Bottom names, top counts.</p>
<p>That's why bigger denominators mean smaller pieces: cutting a pizza into 8 gives smaller slices than cutting it into 4. And it's why ${fr(1,8)} &lt; ${fr(1,4)} even though 8 &gt; 4.</p>
<p><b>Equivalent fractions</b> are the same amount described with different-sized pieces. ${fr(3,4)} = ${fr(6,8)} = ${fr(15,20)}. You get from one to another by multiplying top and bottom by the same number — which is really multiplying by ${fr(5,5)}, and ${fr(5,5)} is just <b>1</b>. Multiplying by 1 never changes a value, only its appearance.</p>`,
watch:`The denominator can never be 0. ${fr('a',0)} would be asking "how many pieces of size nothing make a whole?" — there's no answer, so it's undefined, not zero.`,
gen(){
  if(Math.random()<0.5){
    const [n,d]=pick([[3,4],[2,3],[5,6],[3,8],[4,5],[7,10],[2,5],[5,8],[1,6],[3,5]]);
    const k=ri(2,9),nd=d*k,nn=n*k;
    return{q:`Fill in the missing numerator: &nbsp;<b>${fr(n,d)} = ${fr('?',nd)}</b>`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(nn),accepts:A(String(nn)),
      steps:[
        [`Look at the denominators: ${d} became ${nd}. Ask what ${d} was multiplied by: ${nd} \u00f7 ${d} = <b>${k}</b>.`,
         `The denominator is the piece <i>size</i>, so a change there means you re-cut the whole into ${k} times as many pieces. Figure out the re-cut before you touch the top.`],
        [`Multiply the numerator by that same ${k}: ${n} \u00b7 ${k} = <b>${nn}</b>.`,
         `Every original piece got sliced into ${k} smaller ones, so your ${n} pieces became ${nn} pieces. Same amount of pizza, more cuts.`],
        [`So ${fr(n,d)} = ${fr(nn,nd)}.`,
         `The reason this is legal: multiplying top and bottom by ${k} is multiplying by ${fr(k,k)}, and ${fr(k,k)} = 1. Multiplying by 1 changes how a number <i>looks</i>, never what it <i>is</i>. That one fact powers this entire chapter.`]
      ]};
  }
  const g=ri(2,7),a=ri(2,7)*g,b=(ri(2,5)+ri(1,4))*g;
  const tot=a+b,[sn,sd]=simp(a,tot);
  const thing=pick([['students','ride the bus'],['marbles','are blue'],['books','are hardcover'],['cars','are electric']]);
  return{q:`A box holds ${tot} ${thing[0]}. ${a} of them ${thing[1]}. What <b>fraction</b> ${thing[1]}? <span style="opacity:.6">(lowest terms)</span>`,
    hint:'like 3/8',ans:fr(sn,sd),accepts:A(fracForms(sn,sd),fracForms(a,tot)),
    steps:[
      [`The <b>whole group</b> is ${tot}, so ${tot} goes on the bottom: ${fr('?',tot)}`,
       `The denominator always names the whole you're comparing to. Here the whole is every item in the box, so the total is what the pieces are counted against.`],
      [`The <b>part you're describing</b> is ${a}, so that goes on top: ${fr(a,tot)}`,
       `Top counts, bottom names. You're counting the ${a} that ${thing[1]} out of the ${tot} that exist.`],
      [`Simplify: the largest number dividing both ${a} and ${tot} is <b>${gcd(a,tot)}</b>, giving ${fr(a,tot)} = <b>${fr(sn,sd)}</b>.`,
       `Dividing top and bottom by ${gcd(a,tot)} is dividing by ${fr(gcd(a,tot),gcd(a,tot))} = 1, so the amount is untouched — you've just described it with bigger, fewer pieces. ${sd<10?`Now it's readable: roughly ${sn} out of every ${sd}.`:`Lowest terms is the standard way to report a fraction so two people always write the same answer.`}`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- 19. prime factorization & simplifying ---------- */
T({unit:'Fractions',short:'Primes & simplifying',
title:'Prime factorization and simplifying fractions',
idea:`<p>A <b>prime</b> number has exactly two factors: 1 and itself. 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13 \u2026 Primes are the atoms of arithmetic — every whole number bigger than 1 is built by multiplying primes, and there's <b>only one way</b> to build each number.</p>
<p>That uniqueness is what makes prime factorization so useful. Once you know 84 = 2 \u00b7 2 \u00b7 3 \u00b7 7, you know every factor it could possibly have, because factors are just combinations of those atoms.</p>
<p><b>Simplifying a fraction</b> is finding factors the top and bottom share and cancelling them. Cancelling is legal because a factor over itself equals 1: ${fr(2,2)} = 1, and multiplying by 1 changes nothing. You aren't deleting numbers, you're recognizing hidden 1's.</p>
<p>Divisibility shortcuts worth memorizing: even \u2192 divisible by 2. Digits sum to a multiple of 3 \u2192 divisible by 3. Ends in 0 or 5 \u2192 divisible by 5.</p>`,
watch:`You can only cancel <b>factors</b> — things being multiplied. You cannot cancel across a plus sign. In ${fr('2+6','2')} the top is not 2 times something, it's 2 plus something, so that 2 is not cancellable.`,
gen(){
  if(Math.random()<0.45){
    let n=1;const ps=[2,2,2,3,3,5,7,11];
    const cnt=ri(3,4);for(let i=0;i<cnt;i++)n*=pick(ps);
    if(n<24)n*=2;
    const f=pfac(n),st=[];let cur=n;
    for(let i=0;i<f.length;i++){
      const p=f[i],nx=cur/p;
      const why=p===2?`${cm(cur)} is even, so 2 divides it. Always strip out every 2 you can before moving on \u2014 it's the easiest prime to spot.`
        :p===3?`The digits of ${cm(cur)} add to ${String(cur).split('').reduce((a,b)=>a+ +b,0)}, which is a multiple of 3, so 3 divides ${cm(cur)}.`
        :p===5?`${cm(cur)} ends in ${String(cur).slice(-1)}, so 5 divides it.`
        :`${cm(cur)} has no factors of 2, 3, or 5 left, so test the next primes in order. ${p} divides it evenly.`;
      st.push([`${cm(cur)} \u00f7 ${p} = <b>${cm(nx)}</b>`,why]);cur=nx;
    }
    st.push([`${cur} is <b>prime</b> \u2014 nothing left to break apart.`,
      `You stop when the last quotient is prime, because a prime can't be split any further. Every number you divided out along the way is one of the atoms.`]);
    return{q:`Find the <b>prime factorization</b> of <b>${cm(n)}</b>.`,hint:'like 2*2*3*5',ans:pfStr(n),accepts:pfAcc(n),steps:st};
  }
  const g=ri(3,12),sn=ri(2,9),sd=ri(2,9)+sn;
  const n=sn*g,d=sd*g,[a,b]=simp(n,d);
  return{q:`Simplify to lowest terms: &nbsp;<b>${fr(n,d)}</b>`,hint:'like 3/4',ans:fr(a,b),accepts:A(fracForms(a,b)),
    steps:[
      [`Break both into primes: ${n} = ${pfPlain(n)} &nbsp;and&nbsp; ${d} = ${pfPlain(d)}.`,
       `Prime factorization shows you every factor each number has, so no shared factor can hide from you. It's slower than guessing but it never leaves the fraction half-simplified.`],
      [`The primes appearing in <b>both</b> lists multiply to <b>${gcd(n,d)}</b> \u2014 that's the greatest common factor.`,
       `Anything in both lists is a factor of both numbers, so it can be pulled out of each. Taking all of them at once is what gets you to lowest terms in a single move.`],
      [`Divide top and bottom by ${gcd(n,d)}: &nbsp;${n} \u00f7 ${gcd(n,d)} = ${a}, &nbsp;${d} \u00f7 ${gcd(n,d)} = ${b} &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; <b>${fr(a,b)}</b>`,
       `This is multiplication by ${fr(gcd(n,d),gcd(n,d))} = 1 running in reverse. The value of the fraction is identical; you've just switched to fewer, larger pieces.`],
      [`${b===1?`The denominator became 1, so this fraction was really the whole number <b>${a}</b> all along.`:`${a} and ${b} share no primes now, so <b>${fr(a,b)}</b> is fully simplified.`}`,
       `${b===1?`A denominator of 1 means the whole was cut into one piece \u2014 not cut at all.`:`That's the test for lowest terms: no prime appears on both floors. If one did, you'd still have a hidden 1 to pull out.`}`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- 20. multiplying & dividing fractions ---------- */
T({unit:'Fractions',short:'Multiply & divide fractions',
title:'Multiplying and dividing fractions',
idea:`<p><b>Multiplying is the easy one</b> — straight across, top times top and bottom times bottom. No common denominator needed, and here's why: multiplying fractions means taking a <b>part of a part</b>. ${fr(1,2)} of ${fr(1,3)} is one third cut in half, which is ${fr(1,6)}. You're subdividing, not combining, so the pieces were never required to match.</p>
<p>The word <b>"of" means multiply</b>. ${fr(2,3)} of 18 is ${fr(2,3)} \u00b7 18 = 12. That one translation shows up constantly in percent problems later.</p>
<p><b>Dividing</b> is multiplying by the <b>reciprocal</b> — flip the second fraction and multiply. Keep, change, flip. Why does flipping work? Because a reciprocal is the number that multiplies to 1: ${fr(3,4)} \u00b7 ${fr(4,3)} = ${fr(12,12)} = 1. Multiplying by ${fr(4,3)} is precisely the move that undoes ${fr(3,4)}, and undoing is what division means.</p>
<p>Also worth hearing plainly: 6 \u00f7 ${fr(1,2)} = 12 asks "how many halves fit in 6?" Twelve. Dividing by a number less than 1 makes things <b>bigger</b>, and that's not a paradox once you hear the question correctly.</p>`,
watch:`Cancel <b>before</b> you multiply, not after. It's the same answer either way, but cancelling first keeps the numbers small and saves you from simplifying something like ${fr(126,378)} at the end.`,
gen(){
  if(Math.random()<0.5){
    const a=ri(2,9),b=ri(3,12),c=ri(2,9),d=ri(3,12);
    const rn=a*c,rd=b*d,[sn,sd]=simp(rn,rd),g=gcd(rn,rd);
    return{q:`Multiply: &nbsp;<b>${fr(a,b)} \u00b7 ${fr(c,d)}</b>`,hint:'like 3/8',ans:sd===1?String(sn):fr(sn,sd),accepts:A(fracForms(sn,sd)),
      steps:[
        [`Multiply across the top: ${a} \u00b7 ${c} = <b>${rn}</b>.`,
         `The numerators are counts of pieces, and you're taking ${a} pieces' worth of a thing ${c} pieces big, so the counts multiply. No matching of denominators needed \u2014 nothing is being combined.`],
        [`Multiply across the bottom: ${b} \u00b7 ${d} = <b>${rd}</b>.`,
         `The denominators are piece sizes. Cutting into ${b} parts and then cutting each of those into ${d} parts leaves ${rd} parts total. That's why the bottoms multiply too.`],
        [`So far: ${fr(rn,rd)}.`,
         `Read it back as a sentence: ${fr(a,b)} of ${fr(c,d)} is ${fr(rn,rd)} of the original whole. The arithmetic matches the picture.`],
        g>1?[`Simplify by ${g}: <b>${sd===1?sn:fr(sn,sd)}</b>.`,
          `${rn} and ${rd} share a factor of ${g}, and a factor over itself is 1. Cancelling it changes the appearance, not the amount. Doing this <i>before</i> multiplying would have kept the numbers smaller.`]
         :[`${rn} and ${rd} share no factors, so <b>${fr(rn,rd)}</b> is already in lowest terms.`,
          `Nothing to cancel means no hidden 1's are lurking. You're done.`]
      ]};
  }
  const a=ri(2,9),b=ri(3,12),c=ri(2,9),d=ri(3,12);
  const rn=a*d,rd=b*c,[sn,sd]=simp(rn,rd),g=gcd(rn,rd);
  return{q:`Divide: &nbsp;<b>${fr(a,b)} \u00f7 ${fr(c,d)}</b>`,hint:'like 3/8',ans:sd===1?String(sn):fr(sn,sd),accepts:A(fracForms(sn,sd)),
    steps:[
      [`<b>Keep</b> ${fr(a,b)}, <b>change</b> \u00f7 to \u00b7, <b>flip</b> ${fr(c,d)} into its reciprocal ${fr(d,c)}: &nbsp; ${fr(a,b)} \u00b7 ${fr(d,c)}`,
       `The reciprocal of ${fr(c,d)} is the number that multiplies it to 1: ${fr(c,d)} \u00b7 ${fr(d,c)} = ${fr(c*d,c*d)} = 1. Since division asks you to undo a multiplication, multiplying by the thing that cancels it is exactly the right move.`],
      [`Now it's an ordinary multiplication. Top: ${a} \u00b7 ${d} = <b>${rn}</b>.`,
       `Once you've flipped, every rule from the multiplication side applies with no changes. That's the whole reason we convert \u2014 one procedure instead of two.`],
      [`Bottom: ${b} \u00b7 ${c} = <b>${rd}</b>. &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; ${fr(rn,rd)}`,
       `Straight across again, denominators multiplying because piece sizes compound.`],
      g>1?[`Simplify by ${g}: <b>${sd===1?sn:fr(sn,sd)}</b>.`,
        `A shared factor of ${g} on both floors is a hidden ${fr(g,g)} = 1. Pull it out and the fraction is in lowest terms.`]
       :[`No shared factors &rarr; <b>${sd===1?sn:fr(sn,sd)}</b> is already lowest terms.`,
        `${sn>sd&&sd!==1?`Notice the answer is bigger than 1 \u2014 that happens whenever you divide by something smaller than the number you started with.`:`Nothing cancels, so you're finished.`}`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- 21. adding & subtracting fractions ---------- */
T({unit:'Fractions',short:'Add & subtract fractions',
title:'Adding and subtracting fractions',
idea:`<p>This is the one that needs a common denominator, and here is exactly why: <b>you can only count things that are the same size.</b></p>
<p>${fr(1,2)} + ${fr(1,3)} can't be done as written for the same reason you can't add 1 nickel and 1 dime and say "2 coins" when someone asks how much money you have. The pieces are different sizes, so there's no single unit to count in. Re-cut both into sixths and now they're the same size: ${fr(3,6)} + ${fr(2,6)} = ${fr(5,6)}. Five pieces, each a sixth.</p>
<p><b>You add the numerators only.</b> ${fr(3,6)} + ${fr(2,6)} is ${fr(5,6)}, not ${fr(5,12)}. The denominator isn't a number being added — it's the <i>name</i> of what you're counting. Three sixths plus two sixths is five sixths, the same way 3 feet plus 2 feet is 5 feet, not 5 square feet.</p>
<p>The <b>LCD</b> is the smallest size both fractions can be re-cut into, which is the least common multiple of the denominators. Any common multiple works; the least one just keeps the numbers small.</p>`,
watch:`Contrast this with multiplying, where you <i>don't</i> need a common denominator. Adding combines pieces, so they must match. Multiplying subdivides pieces, so they don't have to. Different jobs, different requirements.`,
gen(){
  const sub=Math.random()<0.42;
  let b,d,a,c,g=0;
  do{b=ri(2,12);d=ri(2,12);}while((b===d||lcm(b,d)>72)&&g++<200);
  const L=lcm(b,d),kb=L/b,kd=L/d;
  if(sub){a=ri(1,b-1);c=ri(1,d-1);if(a*kb<c*kd){[a,c]=[c,a];[b,d]=[d,b];}}
  else{a=ri(1,b-1);c=ri(1,d-1);}
  const KB=L/b,KD=L/d,na=a*KB,nc=c*KD;
  const rn=sub?na-nc:na+nc,[sn,sd]=simp(rn,L),gg=gcd(rn,L);
  const impr=sn>sd&&sd!==1;
  return{q:`${sub?'Subtract':'Add'}: &nbsp;<b>${fr(a,b)} ${sub?'\u2212':'+'} ${fr(c,d)}</b>`,hint:'like 5/6',ans:sd===1?String(sn):fr(sn,sd),accepts:A(fracForms(sn,sd)),
    steps:[
      [`The pieces are different sizes (${b}ths versus ${d}ths), so they can't be counted together yet. Find the <b>LCD</b> \u2014 the least common multiple of ${b} and ${d} \u2014 which is <b>${L}</b>.`,
       `${L===b*d?`${b} and ${d} share no factors, so the smallest size both can be re-cut into is their product, ${L}.`:`${L} is smaller than ${b} \u00b7 ${d} = ${b*d} because ${b} and ${d} share a factor. Using the least common multiple keeps the numerators manageable.`} Any common multiple would work \u2014 the "least" is a convenience, not a requirement.`],
      [`Re-cut the first: multiply top and bottom by ${KB} &rarr; ${fr(a,b)} = <b>${fr(na,L)}</b>.`,
       `Multiplying by ${fr(KB,KB)} is multiplying by 1, so the amount is untouched. Each of the original ${b}ths got split into ${KB} smaller pieces, so ${a} pieces became ${na}.`],
      [`Re-cut the second: multiply top and bottom by ${KD} &rarr; ${fr(c,d)} = <b>${fr(nc,L)}</b>.`,
       `Same reasoning, different re-cut. Now both fractions are counted in ${L}ths, and ${L}ths are ${L}ths no matter which fraction they came from.`],
      [`${sub?'Subtract':'Add'} the <b>numerators only</b>: ${na} ${sub?'\u2212':'+'} ${nc} = <b>${rn}</b>, over ${L} &rarr; ${fr(rn,L)}`,
       `The denominator stays ${L} because it's the <i>name</i> of the piece, not a quantity being combined. ${na} ${L}ths ${sub?'minus':'plus'} ${nc} ${L}ths is ${rn} ${L}ths \u2014 the pieces never changed size, only how many you have.`],
      gg>1?[`Simplify by ${gg} &rarr; <b>${sd===1?sn:fr(sn,sd)}</b>${impr?`, or ${mx(Math.floor(sn/sd),sn%sd,sd)} as a mixed number`:''}.`,
        `${rn} and ${L} share a factor of ${gg}, which is a hidden ${fr(gg,gg)} = 1. Cancel it and you're in lowest terms.`]
       :[`No common factors &rarr; <b>${sd===1?sn:fr(sn,sd)}</b> is already in lowest terms${impr?`, or ${mx(Math.floor(sn/sd),sn%sd,sd)} as a mixed number`:''}.`,
        `${impr?`The numerator is bigger than the denominator, which just means you have more than one whole \u2014 perfectly legal, and often the more useful form to leave it in.`:`Nothing cancels, so this is the final answer.`}`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- 22. mixed numbers ---------- */
T({unit:'Fractions',short:'Mixed numbers',
title:'Mixed numbers and improper fractions',
idea:`<p>A <b>mixed number</b> like 3${fr(1,4)} is secretly an addition: it means 3 + ${fr(1,4)}. An <b>improper fraction</b> like ${fr(13,4)} is the same amount written entirely in quarters. Neither is wrong; they're two dialects for one quantity.</p>
<p><b>Mixed \u2192 improper:</b> multiply the whole number by the denominator, add the numerator. Why? Because each whole contains ${4} quarters, so 3 wholes is 12 quarters, plus the 1 you already had = 13 quarters. You're just converting everything into the same unit.</p>
<p><b>Improper \u2192 mixed:</b> divide. ${fr(13,4)} means 13 \u00f7 4 = 3 remainder 1, so 3 wholes with 1 quarter left over. That leftover remainder is precisely the new numerator — the same remainder from long division back in Chapter 1, finally given a proper home.</p>
<p>For arithmetic, <b>convert to improper first</b>. Multiplying 2${fr(1,3)} \u00b7 1${fr(1,2)} by trying to handle wholes and parts separately is a trap; as ${fr(7,3)} \u00b7 ${fr(3,2)} it's routine.</p>`,
watch:`3${fr(1,4)} means 3 <b>plus</b> ${fr(1,4)}, not 3 times ${fr(1,4)}. It's the one place in all of math where two things written side by side aren't being multiplied — which is exactly why you should convert to improper before doing anything with it.`,
gen(){
  const r=Math.random();
  if(r<0.34){
    const d=ri(3,9),w=ri(2,9),n=ri(1,d-1),imp=w*d+n;
    return{q:`Write as an <b>improper fraction</b>: &nbsp;<b>${mx(w,n,d)}</b>`,hint:'like 13/4',ans:fr(imp,d),accepts:A(fracForms(imp,d),[imp+'/'+d]),
      steps:[
        [`${mx(w,n,d)} means ${w} + ${fr(n,d)}. Everything has to become ${d}ths.`,
         `You can't write one number as a fraction while part of it is still a whole. Converting to a single unit \u2014 ${d}ths \u2014 is the entire task.`],
        [`Each whole holds ${d} of the ${d}ths, so ${w} wholes = ${w} \u00b7 ${d} = <b>${w*d}</b> of them.`,
         `This is why you multiply the whole number by the denominator. It's a unit conversion, the same as turning ${w} feet into ${w*12} inches.`],
        [`Add the ${n} you already had: ${w*d} + ${n} = <b>${imp}</b>, all over ${d} &rarr; <b>${fr(imp,d)}</b>.`,
         `The denominator never changes, because the piece size never changed. You only recounted how many pieces there are.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.67){
    const d=ri(3,9),w=ri(2,9),n=ri(1,d-1),imp=w*d+n;
    return{q:`Write as a <b>mixed number</b>: &nbsp;<b>${fr(imp,d)}</b>`,hint:'like 3 1/4',ans:mx(w,n,d),accepts:A(`${w} ${n}/${d}`,`${w}-${n}/${d}`,`${imp}/${d}`),
      steps:[
        [`A fraction bar is a division sign: ${fr(imp,d)} means <b>${imp} \u00f7 ${d}</b>.`,
         `This is the definition of a fraction, not a trick. Once you see the division, the conversion is just long division from Chapter 1.`],
        [`${imp} \u00f7 ${d} = <b>${w}</b> with a remainder of <b>${n}</b>.`,
         `${d} fits into ${imp} a total of ${w} times, using up ${w*d}. What's left, ${n}, couldn't make another full group.`],
        [`The quotient ${w} is the whole part; the remainder ${n} becomes the numerator over the same ${d} &rarr; <b>${mx(w,n,d)}</b>.`,
         `Back in Chapter 1 that remainder was leftover scrap. Now you can name it: ${n} pieces out of ${d}, which is ${fr(n,d)} of a whole. Nothing gets discarded anymore.`]
      ]};
  }
  const d1=ri(2,6),d2=ri(2,6),w1=ri(1,5),n1=ri(1,d1-1),w2=ri(1,5),n2=ri(1,d2-1);
  const i1=w1*d1+n1,i2=w2*d2+n2,rn=i1*i2,rd=d1*d2,[sn,sd]=simp(rn,rd);
  const W=Math.floor(sn/sd),R=sn%sd;
  return{q:`Multiply: &nbsp;<b>${mx(w1,n1,d1)} \u00b7 ${mx(w2,n2,d2)}</b>`,hint:'like 3 1/4',
    ans:sd===1?String(sn):(R?mx(W,R,sd):String(W)),accepts:A(fracForms(sn,sd),sd!==1&&R?[`${W} ${R}/${sd}`,`${W}-${R}/${sd}`]:[]),
    steps:[
      [`Convert both to improper first: ${mx(w1,n1,d1)} = ${fr(i1,d1)} &nbsp;and&nbsp; ${mx(w2,n2,d2)} = ${fr(i2,d2)}.`,
       `Never multiply mixed numbers as they stand. ${mx(w1,n1,d1)} is a hidden <i>sum</i>, and multiplying two sums means every part has to meet every other part \u2014 far more work, and far more chances to drop a piece.`],
      [`Multiply straight across: top ${i1} \u00b7 ${i2} = <b>${rn}</b>, bottom ${d1} \u00b7 ${d2} = <b>${rd}</b> &rarr; ${fr(rn,rd)}`,
       `Now it's just the multiplication rule from the last topic. Converting bought you a problem you already know how to do.`],
      [`Simplify: ${fr(rn,rd)} = <b>${sd===1?sn:fr(sn,sd)}</b>.`,
       `${gcd(rn,rd)>1?`Divide top and bottom by ${gcd(rn,rd)} \u2014 a hidden 1 \u2014 to reach lowest terms.`:`No shared factors, so it's already in lowest terms.`}`],
      [`${sd===1?`The denominator is 1, so the answer is the whole number <b>${sn}</b>.`:R?`Convert back to a mixed number: ${sn} \u00f7 ${sd} = ${W} remainder ${R} &rarr; <b>${mx(W,R,sd)}</b>.`:`${sn} \u00f7 ${sd} comes out even &rarr; <b>${W}</b>.`}`,
       `Going back to mixed form is optional but usually kinder to read \u2014 ${sd===1?`a whole number needs no fraction at all`:R?`"about ${W} and a bit" is easier to picture than "${sn} ${sd}ths"`:`it turned out to be a whole number`}. Both forms are correct answers.`]
    ]};
}});

/* ============================================================
   UNIT 5 — DECIMALS
   ============================================================ */
const D_NAMES=['','tenths','hundredths','thousandths'];
function decOf(intVal,places){return (intVal/Math.pow(10,places)).toFixed(places)}
function trimz(s){return s.indexOf('.')<0?s:s.replace(/0+$/,'').replace(/\.$/,'')}

T({unit:'Decimals',short:'Add & subtract decimals',
title:'Decimals: place value, adding and subtracting',
idea:`<p>Decimals are <b>fractions in disguise</b> — specifically, fractions whose denominators are powers of ten. 0.7 is ${fr(7,10)}. 0.43 is ${fr(43,100)}. 0.006 is ${fr(6,1000)}.</p>
<p>The seating chart from Chapter 1 just keeps going to the right, and each new seat is worth <b>one tenth</b> of the one before it: ones, then tenths, then hundredths, then thousandths. The decimal point isn't a seat — it's a <b>marker</b> that tells you where the ones place ends.</p>
<p>So adding and subtracting decimals uses the identical rule as whole numbers: <b>line up by place value</b>. With whole numbers you did that by shoving everything right. With decimals you do it by <b>lining up the decimal points</b>, which automatically puts tenths over tenths and hundredths over hundredths.</p>
<p>And you may pad with zeros freely. 4.7 = 4.70 = 4.700, because those zeros are claiming "no hundredths, no thousandths" — true statements that add nothing.</p>`,
watch:`Lining up the <i>right edges</i> works for whole numbers but destroys decimals. In 4.7 + 12.35, right-edge alignment would stack the 7 tenths under the 5 hundredths. Line up the <b>points</b>, then pad.`,
gen(){
  const p1=ri(1,2),p2=ri(1,2),mp=Math.max(p1,p2);
  let F=[ri(25,9990),p1],G=[ri(25,9990),p2];
  const sc=z=>z[0]*Math.pow(10,mp-z[1]);
  const sub=Math.random()<0.42;
  if(sub&&sc(G)>sc(F)){const t=F;F=G;G=t;}
  const av=decOf(F[0],F[1]),bv=decOf(G[0],G[1]);
  const res=sub?sc(F)-sc(G):sc(F)+sc(G),rv=decOf(res,mp);
  const padA=mp-F[1],padB=mp-G[1];
  return{q:`${sub?'Subtract':'Add'}: &nbsp;<b>${av} ${sub?'\u2212':'+'} ${bv}</b>`,hint:'a decimal',ans:rv,
    accepts:A(decForms(rv),decForms(trimz(rv))),
    steps:[
      [`Stack them with the <b>decimal points aligned</b>, one directly above the other.`,
       `The point marks where the ones place ends, so aligning the points forces tenths over tenths and hundredths over hundredths. That's the same "only combine matching units" rule you've used since whole-number addition \u2014 nothing new, just applied to smaller seats.`],
      (padA||padB)
        ? [`Pad the shorter one with zeros so both have ${mp} decimal place${mp>1?'s':''}: &nbsp;<b>${(+av).toFixed(mp)}</b> ${sub?'\u2212':'+'} <b>${(+bv).toFixed(mp)}</b>`,
           `Those zeros don't change either value \u2014 they announce "zero ${D_NAMES[mp]}," which is true and adds nothing. What they <i>do</i> give you is a full ${mp}th column so no seat is left empty during the ${sub?'borrowing':'carrying'}.`]
        : [`Both already have ${mp} decimal place${mp>1?'s':''}, so every column is full.`,
           `No padding needed. Every seat has a digit in it and the columns are ready to combine.`],
      [`Now ${sub?'subtract':'add'} column by column exactly like whole numbers, ${sub?'borrowing':'carrying'} across the decimal point as if it weren't there: <b>${trimz(rv)}</b>`,
       `The point doesn't block ${sub?'borrowing':'carrying'}. One tenth is still ten hundredths, and one whole is still ten tenths \u2014 the ratio between neighboring seats is 10 everywhere on the line, so the trading works identically on both sides of the point.`],
      [`Bring the decimal point <b>straight down</b> into the answer &rarr; <b>${rv}</b>.`,
       `It drops straight down because the columns never moved. Every digit in your answer came from a column whose place value you fixed at the very start.`]
    ]};
}});

T({unit:'Decimals',short:'Multiply & divide decimals',
title:'Multiplying and dividing decimals',
idea:`<p><b>Multiplying:</b> ignore the decimal points completely, multiply as whole numbers, then count. The number of decimal places in the answer equals the <b>total</b> in the two factors.</p>
<p>Why counting works: 3.4 \u00b7 2.7 is really ${fr(34,10)} \u00b7 ${fr(27,10)} = ${fr(918,100)} = 9.18. Each decimal place is a division by ten. One place plus one place is two divisions by ten, which is a division by 100 \u2014 two places. You're not memorizing a counting rule, you're tracking how many tens ended up in the denominator.</p>
<p><b>Dividing:</b> you need a whole-number divisor, so slide the decimal point right in <b>both</b> numbers until the divisor is whole. 8.4 \u00f7 0.4 becomes 84 \u00f7 4 = 21.</p>
<p>Why that's legal: division is a fraction, and moving both points one place is multiplying top and bottom by 10 \u2014 which is multiplying by ${fr(10,10)} = 1. Same value, friendlier form. It's the identical "multiply by a form of 1" move you used on equivalent fractions.</p>`,
watch:`Multiplying by a number <b>less than 1 makes things smaller</b>: 40 \u00b7 0.5 = 20. And dividing by a number less than 1 makes things <b>bigger</b>: 40 \u00f7 0.5 = 80, because you're asking how many halves fit in 40. If your answer moved the wrong direction, check the decimal point before anything else.`,
gen(){
  if(Math.random()<0.5){
    const p1=ri(1,2),p2=ri(1,2);
    const i1=ri(12,99)*(p1===2?1:1),i2=ri(12,99);
    const v1=decOf(i1,p1),v2=decOf(i2,p2);
    const prod=i1*i2,tp=p1+p2,pv=decOf(prod,tp);
    return{q:`Multiply: &nbsp;<b>${v1} \u00d7 ${v2}</b>`,hint:'a decimal',ans:pv,accepts:A(decForms(pv),decForms(trimz(pv))),
      steps:[
        [`Ignore both decimal points for now and multiply as whole numbers: ${i1} \u00d7 ${i2} = <b>${cm(prod)}</b>.`,
         `You're allowed to do this because ${v1} = ${fr(i1,Math.pow(10,p1))} and ${v2} = ${fr(i2,Math.pow(10,p2))}. Multiplying fractions goes straight across, so the numerators ${i1} and ${i2} multiply on their own, with no interference from the denominators.`],
        [`Count decimal places in the factors: ${v1} has <b>${p1}</b>, ${v2} has <b>${p2}</b>. Total = <b>${tp}</b>.`,
         `Those denominators were 10<sup>${p1}</sup> and 10<sup>${p2}</sup>, and they multiply to 10<sup>${tp}</sup> = ${cm(Math.pow(10,tp))}. The count isn't a rule to memorize \u2014 it's just bookkeeping on how many tens landed underneath.`],
        [`Put ${tp} decimal place${tp>1?'s':''} into ${cm(prod)} &rarr; <b>${pv}</b>.`,
         `${cm(prod)} \u00f7 ${cm(Math.pow(10,tp))} = ${pv}. Dividing by a power of ten slides the point left, which is exactly what placing the decimal does.`],
        [`<b>Sanity check:</b> ${v1} is about ${Math.round(+v1)} and ${v2} is about ${Math.round(+v2)}, so expect roughly ${cm(Math.round(+v1)*Math.round(+v2))}. Got ${pv}. \u2713`,
         `Estimating catches misplaced decimal points instantly \u2014 and a misplaced point is the error that costs the most, since it's off by a factor of ten.`]
      ]};
  }
  const dp=ri(1,2),q0=ri(3,49);
  const dInt=ri(2,9)*(dp===1?1:ri(2,9));
  const dVal=decOf(dInt,dp);
  const nInt=dInt*q0,nVal=decOf(nInt,dp);
  return{q:`Divide: &nbsp;<b>${nVal} \u00f7 ${dVal}</b>`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(q0),accepts:A(String(q0),cm(q0)),
    steps:[
      [`Write it as a fraction: ${fr(nVal,dVal)}. The divisor ${dVal} has ${dp} decimal place${dp>1?'s':''}, so slide <b>both</b> points ${dp} place${dp>1?'s':''} right.`,
       `You want a whole-number divisor because long division only knows how to count whole groups. Fixing the divisor is the goal; the dividend just has to come along.`],
      [`Both numbers get multiplied by ${cm(Math.pow(10,dp))}: &nbsp;${nVal} &rarr; <b>${cm(nInt)}</b> and ${dVal} &rarr; <b>${dInt}</b>.`,
       `Multiplying top and bottom by ${cm(Math.pow(10,dp))} is multiplying by ${fr(cm(Math.pow(10,dp)),cm(Math.pow(10,dp)))} = 1. The quotient is untouched \u2014 same problem, easier clothes. This is the exact move you used to build equivalent fractions.`],
      [`Now do ordinary long division: ${cm(nInt)} \u00f7 ${dInt} = <b>${cm(q0)}</b>.`,
       `Whole divisor, whole dividend, no decimals anywhere. The four moves from Chapter 1 \u2014 divide, multiply, subtract, bring down \u2014 work unchanged.`],
      [`<b>Check:</b> ${cm(q0)} \u00d7 ${dVal} = ${nVal} \u2713`,
       `Multiplying back confirms both the arithmetic and that you slid the points the same number of places on both numbers.`]
    ]};
}});

T({unit:'Decimals',short:'Fractions \u2194 decimals',
title:'Converting between fractions and decimals',
idea:`<p>They're the same numbers in different notation, and the bridge between them is <b>division</b>.</p>
<p><b>Fraction \u2192 decimal:</b> just divide. ${fr(3,8)} <i>means</i> 3 \u00f7 8, so do that division and you get 0.375. Add a decimal point and as many zeros as you need to the 3 — appending zeros after the point doesn't change its value, it just gives you something to bring down.</p>
<p><b>Decimal \u2192 fraction:</b> read the last place out loud and you've already said the denominator. 0.45 is "forty-five hundredths," which is ${fr(45,100)}. Then simplify: ${fr(9,20)}.</p>
<p>Some fractions terminate and some repeat forever. ${fr(1,3)} = 0.333\u2026 because 3 never divides evenly into 10, 100, or any power of ten. A fraction terminates only when its denominator, in lowest terms, is built from nothing but <b>2's and 5's</b> — the primes that make up ten.</p>`,
watch:`Don't read the fraction bar as anything but division. ${fr(3,8)} is 3 \u00f7 8 = 0.375, not 8 \u00f7 3 = 2.666\u2026 The <b>top goes inside</b> the division house.`,
gen(){
  if(Math.random()<0.5){
    const [n,d]=pick([[3,8],[1,4],[5,8],[3,4],[7,8],[1,8],[2,5],[3,5],[9,20],[7,10],[13,20],[1,2],[3,16]]);
    const val=n/d,vs=String(val);
    const dec=vs.split('.')[1].length;
    return{q:`Write as a <b>decimal</b>: &nbsp;<b>${fr(n,d)}</b>`,hint:'a decimal',ans:vs,accepts:A(decForms(vs)),
      steps:[
        [`The fraction bar is a division sign: ${fr(n,d)} means <b>${n} \u00f7 ${d}</b>, with ${n} inside.`,
         `Top divided by bottom, always. The numerator is the amount being split up; the denominator is how many parts you're splitting it into.`],
        [`${d} doesn't fit into ${n}, so write ${n} as ${n}.000 and divide: <b>${n} \u00f7 ${d} = ${vs}</b>.`,
         `Those zeros after the point are free \u2014 ${n} and ${n}.000 are the same number. They exist purely to give long division digits to bring down, so you can keep going past the ones place into tenths, hundredths and beyond.`],
        [`Read it back: ${vs} is ${(val*Math.pow(10,dec))} ${D_NAMES[dec]||'parts out of '+Math.pow(10,dec)}.`,
         `And that's a fair check: ${fr(n*(Math.pow(10,dec)/d),Math.pow(10,dec))} really does reduce to ${fr(n,d)}, so the decimal and the fraction name the same amount.`]
      ]};
  }
  const vs=pick(['0.35','0.8','0.24','0.75','0.6','0.45','0.125','0.28','0.15','0.375','0.52']);
  const dec=vs.split('.')[1].length,den=Math.pow(10,dec),num=Math.round(+vs*den);
  const [a,b]=simp(num,den);
  return{q:`Write as a <b>fraction</b> in lowest terms: &nbsp;<b>${vs}</b>`,hint:'like 3/8',ans:fr(a,b),accepts:A(fracForms(a,b)),
    steps:[
      [`Read the decimal out loud: ${vs} is "<b>${num} ${D_NAMES[dec]}</b>."`,
       `Saying it correctly hands you the fraction for free. The name of the last place <i>is</i> the denominator \u2014 that's what decimal notation was built to do.`],
      [`Write what you just said: <b>${fr(num,den)}</b>.`,
       `${num} on top because that's how many pieces, ${den} on the bottom because that's the size of each piece. The last digit sits in the ${D_NAMES[dec]} place, so ${den} is the denominator.`],
      gcd(num,den)>1
        ? [`Simplify by ${gcd(num,den)}: &nbsp;<b>${fr(a,b)}</b>.`,
           `${num} and ${den} share a factor of ${gcd(num,den)}, which is a hidden ${fr(gcd(num,den),gcd(num,den))} = 1. Cancelling it leaves the same amount in lowest terms.`]
        : [`${num} and ${den} share no factors, so <b>${fr(a,b)}</b> is already lowest terms.`,
           `Nothing to cancel means no hidden 1's. Done.`]
    ]};
}});

