   UNIT 6 — RATIOS, RATES & PROPORTIONS
   ============================================================ */
T({unit:'Ratios & percents',short:'Ratios & rates',
title:'Ratios, rates and unit rates',
idea:`<p>A <b>ratio</b> compares two quantities by division. A <b>rate</b> is a ratio where the two quantities have <b>different units</b> — miles per hour, dollars per pound, students per teacher.</p>
<p>A <b>unit rate</b> is a rate whose bottom has been reduced to <b>1</b>. That's the whole trick, and it's why unit rates are so useful: once the denominator is 1, comparison becomes trivial. $18 for 6 pounds and $22 for 8 pounds is a hard comparison. $3.00 per pound versus $2.75 per pound is not.</p>
<p>To find it, <b>divide by the bottom quantity</b>. You're doing to the ratio exactly what you'd do to any fraction to make its denominator 1 — dividing both parts by the same number, which is multiplying by a form of 1 and leaves the relationship unchanged.</p>
<p>Watch the word <b>"per."</b> It means "for each one," and it's a division sign written in English.</p>`,
watch:`Order matters in a ratio. "3 cats to 5 dogs" is ${fr(3,5)}, and ${fr(5,3)} would be a different claim about the world. Write the units next to the numbers while you work and the order takes care of itself.`,
gen(){
  if(Math.random()<0.55){
    const unit=pick([['pounds of coffee','$'],['gallons of gas','$'],['pounds of apples','$']]);
    const per=pick([2.25,3.50,1.80,4.20,2.60,5.40,1.25]);
    const qty=ri(3,12),tot=Math.round(per*qty*100)/100;
    const ans=(Math.round(per*100)/100).toFixed(2);
    return{q:`${qty} ${unit[0]} cost <b>$${tot.toFixed(2)}</b>. What is the <b>unit rate</b> (cost per one)?`,hint:'like 3.25',ans:'$'+ans,accepts:A(ans,'$'+ans,decForms(String(+ans))),
      steps:[
        [`Write the rate as a fraction with units attached: ${fr('$'+tot.toFixed(2),qty)}.`,
         `Keeping the units in the fraction is what stops you from dividing backwards. Dollars are on top because you want dollars per one \u2014 the thing you want "per" goes on the bottom.`],
        [`"Per one" means the denominator must become <b>1</b>. Divide both parts by ${qty}.`,
         `Dividing top and bottom by the same number is multiplying by ${fr(1,1)}'s cousin \u2014 a form of 1 \u2014 so the rate itself is unchanged. You're renaming the same relationship in terms of a single unit.`],
        [`${tot.toFixed(2)} \u00f7 ${qty} = <b>$${ans}</b> per ${unit[0].replace(/^(pounds|gallons) of /,'').replace(/s$/,'')} unit.`,
         `Now the bottom is 1, so the number on top <i>is</i> the rate. This is the form you can compare against any other price without doing more arithmetic.`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${qty} \u00d7 $${ans} = $${tot.toFixed(2)} \u2713`,
         `Multiplying the unit rate back by the quantity has to return the original total. If it doesn't, you divided the wrong way around.`]
      ]};
  }
  const g=ri(2,9),a=ri(2,9)*g,b=ri(2,9)*g;
  const [sa,sb]=simp(a,b);
  return{q:`A room has ${a} chairs and ${b} tables. Write the <b>ratio of chairs to tables</b> in lowest terms.`,hint:'like 3/5 or 3:5',
    ans:`${sa}:${sb}`,accepts:A(`${sa}:${sb}`,`${sa}/${sb}`,`${sa} to ${sb}`),
    steps:[
      [`"Chairs to tables" fixes the order: chairs first. So it's ${a} to ${b}, written ${fr(a,b)} or ${a}:${b}.`,
       `The order in the sentence is the order in the ratio. Reversing it would be answering a different question \u2014 tables to chairs is a different comparison.`],
      [`Simplify by the greatest common factor, <b>${gcd(a,b)}</b>: &nbsp;${a} \u00f7 ${gcd(a,b)} = ${sa}, ${b} \u00f7 ${gcd(a,b)} = ${sb}.`,
       `A ratio simplifies exactly like a fraction, because it <i>is</i> one. Dividing both parts by ${gcd(a,b)} keeps the comparison identical \u2014 the relationship between chairs and tables hasn't changed, only the numbers describing it.`],
      [`The ratio is <b>${sa}:${sb}</b>.`,
       `Read it as a claim about the world: for every ${sa} chair${sa===1?'':'s'} there ${sb===1?'is':'are'} ${sb} table${sb===1?'':'s'}. That sentence is true for ${a} and ${b} too, which is the point of simplifying \u2014 the small numbers reveal the pattern.`]
    ]};
}});

T({unit:'Ratios & percents',short:'Proportions',
title:'Proportions',
idea:`<p>A <b>proportion</b> is a statement that two ratios are equal: ${fr(3,4)} = ${fr('x',20)}. Whenever a relationship holds steady while the quantities scale up or down, you have a proportion.</p>
<p>There are two honest ways to solve one, and you should know both.</p>
<p><b>Scaling</b>: ask what the denominator was multiplied by, and do the same to the numerator. That's just equivalent fractions from Chapter 4.</p>
<p><b>Cross multiplication</b>: ${fr('a','b')} = ${fr('c','d')} becomes a \u00b7 d = b \u00b7 c. This isn't magic — it's what you get when you multiply <b>both sides</b> by b and by d. The denominators cancel, and you're left with the cross products. It's the balance rule from Chapter 3, applied twice.</p>
<p>Cross multiplication is the reliable one when the scaling factor isn't a whole number.</p>`,
watch:`Set up both ratios in the <b>same order</b>. If the left side is miles over hours, the right side must also be miles over hours. Label the units on both fractions before you cross multiply and mismatched setups become impossible.`,
gen(){
  if(Math.random()<0.5){
    const b=ri(2,9),k=ri(2,9),a=ri(2,9),d=b*k,c=a*k;
    return{q:`Solve for x: &nbsp;<b>${fr(a,b)} = ${fr('x',d)}</b>`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(c),accepts:A(String(c)),
      steps:[
        [`Cross multiply: <b>${a} \u00b7 ${d} = ${b} \u00b7 x</b> &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; ${a*d} = ${b}x`,
         `Where this comes from: multiply both sides by ${b} and by ${d}. On the left the ${b} cancels; on the right the ${d} cancels. What survives is each numerator paired with the opposite denominator \u2014 the cross products.`],
        [`Divide both sides by ${b}: &nbsp;x = ${fr(a*d,b)} = <b>${c}</b>`,
         `Now it's a one-step equation. Undo the multiplication by ${b} with division, done to both sides so the balance holds.`],
        [`<b>Faster route, same answer:</b> ${b} \u00b7 ${k} = ${d}, so the whole fraction scaled up by ${k}. Then ${a} \u00b7 ${k} = <b>${c}</b>.`,
         `When the scale factor is a whole number, this is quicker \u2014 it's just equivalent fractions. Multiplying top and bottom by ${k} is multiplying by ${fr(k,k)} = 1, so the ratio never changed.`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${fr(a,b)} and ${fr(c,d)} \u2014 both reduce to ${fr(...simp(c,d))}. \u2713`,
         `Two fractions that reduce to the same lowest terms are genuinely equal, which is exactly what the proportion claimed.`]
      ]};
  }
  const per=ri(2,9),unitB=ri(2,7),scale=ri(2,8);
  const a=per*scale,b=unitB*scale;
  const thing=pick([['cups of flour','cookies'],['ounces of syrup','drinks'],['pounds of feed','chickens']]);
  return{q:`A recipe uses <b>${per} ${thing[0]}</b> for <b>${unitB} ${thing[1]}</b>. How many ${thing[0]} for <b>${b} ${thing[1]}</b>?`,
    hint:'a whole number',ans:String(a),accepts:A(String(a)),
    steps:[
      [`Set up two ratios in the <b>same order</b> \u2014 ${thing[0]} on top, ${thing[1]} on the bottom, both sides: &nbsp;${fr(per,unitB)} = ${fr('x',b)}`,
       `Matching the order is the whole setup. If one side were flipped you'd be claiming something false about the recipe, and no amount of correct algebra afterward would rescue it.`],
      [`The relationship stays constant, which is what makes this a proportion: more ${thing[1]} needs proportionally more ${thing[0]}.`,
       `Proportions only apply when the relationship really is steady. Doubling the ${thing[1]} genuinely doubles the ${thing[0]} \u2014 that assumption is what licenses the equation.`],
      [`Cross multiply: ${per} \u00b7 ${b} = ${unitB} \u00b7 x &rarr; ${per*b} = ${unitB}x. Divide by ${unitB}: <b>x = ${a}</b>.`,
       `Cross multiplying is multiplying both sides by both denominators, which clears the fractions and leaves a plain one-step equation.`],
      [`<b>Check:</b> ${b} \u00f7 ${unitB} = ${scale}, so the batch scaled up ${scale} times \u2014 and ${per} \u00b7 ${scale} = ${a}. \u2713`,
       `The scaling view confirms the algebra. When both methods agree, you can be confident the setup itself was right.`]
    ]};
}});

