T({unit:'Ratios & percents',short:'Percents',
title:'Percents, decimals and fractions',
idea:`<p><b>Per cent</b> is Latin for <b>per hundred</b>. That's not trivia — it's the definition. 43% <i>is</i> ${fr(43,100)}. A percent is a fraction whose denominator is always 100, wearing a costume.</p>
<p>Once you believe that, both conversions explain themselves.</p>
<p><b>Percent \u2192 decimal:</b> divide by 100, which slides the point <b>two places left</b>. 43% = 43 \u00f7 100 = 0.43. You're not "moving the decimal because someone said so" \u2014 you're carrying out the division the % sign was asking for all along.</p>
<p><b>Decimal \u2192 percent:</b> multiply by 100, sliding the point <b>two places right</b>, then write the %. 0.07 = 7%.</p>
<p><b>Fraction \u2192 percent:</b> divide to get the decimal, then multiply by 100. ${fr(3,8)} = 0.375 = 37.5%.</p>
<p>And percents can exceed 100. 150% = ${fr(150,100)} = 1.5 \u2014 one and a half of the thing. Nothing about "per hundred" caps you at a hundred.</p>`,
watch:`0.5% and 50% are a hundred times apart. 0.5% = 0.005, not 0.5. When a percent already contains a decimal point, do the division carefully instead of eyeballing the shift.`,
gen(){
  const r=Math.random();
  if(r<0.34){
    const p=pick([4,7,12,35,60,125,150,8.5,2.5,0.5,87.5]);
    const v=p/100,vs=String(v);
    return{q:`Write as a <b>decimal</b>: &nbsp;<b>${p}%</b>`,hint:'a decimal',ans:vs,accepts:A(decForms(vs)),
      steps:[
        [`The % sign literally means "divided by 100," so ${p}% = ${fr(p,100)}.`,
         `Translate the symbol first. Once it's a fraction over 100, there's no rule left to remember \u2014 there's just a division to carry out.`],
        [`${p} \u00f7 100 = <b>${vs}</b>, which is the point sliding <b>two places left</b>.`,
         `Dividing by 100 makes every digit worth one hundredth of what it was, so each one moves two seats right of the point. The shortcut and the division are the same act.`],
        [`So ${p}% = <b>${vs}</b>.`,
         `Check it against your sense of size: ${p}% is ${p<100?`less than a whole thing, and ${vs} is less than 1. \u2713`:`more than a whole thing, and ${vs} is more than 1. \u2713`}`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.67){
    const v=pick([0.3,0.07,0.45,0.9,1.2,0.125,0.06,0.875,2.5]);
    const p=+(v*100).toFixed(4),ps=String(p);
    return{q:`Write as a <b>percent</b>: &nbsp;<b>${v}</b>`,hint:'like 45',ans:ps+'%',accepts:A(ps,ps+'%'),
      steps:[
        [`Going to a percent means expressing it <b>out of 100</b>, so multiply by 100.`,
         `A percent must be a count per hundred. Multiplying by 100 re-scales the number so it's counting against a whole of 100 instead of a whole of 1.`],
        [`${v} \u00d7 100 = <b>${ps}</b>, the point sliding <b>two places right</b>.`,
         `Multiplying by 100 makes every digit worth a hundred times more, so each one moves two seats left of the point. Same reasoning as the last conversion, running backwards.`],
        [`Attach the sign: <b>${ps}%</b>.`,
         `Don't drop the %. Without it you're claiming the number is ${ps}, which is ${p>1?'a hundred times':'far'} bigger than ${v}. The symbol is carrying the \u00f7100 and has to stay.`]
      ]};
  }
  const [n,d]=pick([[3,8],[1,4],[3,4],[2,5],[7,10],[5,8],[1,2],[9,20],[3,5],[1,8]]);
  const v=n/d,p=+(v*100).toFixed(4);
  return{q:`Write as a <b>percent</b>: &nbsp;<b>${fr(n,d)}</b>`,hint:'like 45',ans:p+'%',accepts:A(String(p),p+'%'),
    steps:[
      [`First get a decimal \u2014 the fraction bar means divide: ${n} \u00f7 ${d} = <b>${v}</b>.`,
       `You can't go straight from ${d}ths to hundredths by inspection unless ${d} divides 100 nicely. Routing through the decimal always works, whatever the denominator.`],
      [`Now multiply by 100 to express it per hundred: ${v} \u00d7 100 = <b>${p}</b>.`,
       `Two places right. This is re-cutting the whole into 100 pieces and counting how many you'd have \u2014 same idea as finding an equivalent fraction with denominator 100.`],
      [`So ${fr(n,d)} = <b>${p}%</b>.`,
       `Equivalently, ${fr(n,d)} = ${fr(p,100)}. All three notations \u2014 fraction, decimal, percent \u2014 are naming one number.`]
    ]};
}});

T({unit:'Ratios & percents',short:'Percent problems',
title:'Solving percent problems',
idea:`<p>Every percent problem is the same equation with a different blank:</p>
<p style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:18px"><b>amount = percent \u00b7 base</b></p>
<p>The <b>base</b> is the whole thing you're taking a percent <i>of</i>. The <b>amount</b> is the piece you end up with. Find those two words in the sentence and the equation writes itself.</p>
<p>Two translation rules do all the work. <b>"Of" means multiply</b> — it did back in fractions and it still does. <b>"Is" means equals</b>. So "what is 35% of 80" becomes x = 0.35 \u00b7 80 almost word for word.</p>
<p>Always convert the percent to a <b>decimal before multiplying</b>. Multiplying by 35 instead of 0.35 gives an answer a hundred times too large, and it's the most common mistake in this topic.</p>
<p>The proportion form ${fr('part','whole')} = ${fr('p',100)} works just as well and some people find it easier to set up. Same relationship, different arrangement.</p>`,
watch:`A 20% increase followed by a 20% decrease does <b>not</b> return you to the start. $100 grows to $120, then 20% of <i>120</i> is 24, leaving $96. The base changed between the two steps \u2014 always ask what the percent is being taken <i>of</i>.`,
gen(){
  const t=ri(1,3);
  if(t===1){
    const p=pick([15,20,25,30,40,45,60,75,80]),base=pick([40,60,80,120,160,200,240,300,500]);
    const amt=p*base/100;
    return{q:`What is <b>${p}%</b> of <b>${base}</b>?`,hint:'a number',ans:String(amt),accepts:A(String(amt),cm(amt)),
      steps:[
        [`Identify the pieces: the <b>base</b> is ${base} (it follows the word "of"), the <b>percent</b> is ${p}%, and the <b>amount</b> is what you're looking for.`,
         `The word "of" points straight at the base every time. Labelling the three roles before you compute is what keeps you from dividing when you should multiply.`],
        [`Convert the percent to a decimal: ${p}% = ${p} \u00f7 100 = <b>${p/100}</b>.`,
         `Do this before multiplying, always. ${p} \u00b7 ${base} would be ${cm(p*base)} \u2014 a hundred times too big, and the % sign is exactly the \u00f7100 you'd have ignored.`],
        [`Multiply: amount = ${p/100} \u00b7 ${base} = <b>${amt}</b>.`,
         `"Of" means multiply. You're taking ${p/100} of a whole worth ${base}, the same way you'd take ${fr(1,2)} of 80 by multiplying.`],
        [`<b>Sanity check:</b> ${p}% is ${p<50?`less than half`:p===50?`exactly half`:`more than half`} of the whole, and ${amt} is ${p<50?'less':p===50?'exactly':'more'} than half of ${base}. \u2713`,
         `A percent under 100 must give an answer smaller than the base. That single check catches decimal-point errors instantly.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(t===2){
    const base=pick([40,50,80,120,200,160,250]),p=pick([15,20,25,30,40,60,75]),amt=p*base/100;
    return{q:`<b>${amt}</b> is what <b>percent</b> of <b>${base}</b>?`,hint:'like 25',ans:p+'%',accepts:A(String(p),p+'%'),
      steps:[
        [`Roles: the <b>base</b> is ${base} (after "of"), the <b>amount</b> is ${amt}, and the <b>percent</b> is unknown.`,
         `Same three roles as always \u2014 only the blank moved. Notice ${amt} is the piece and ${base} is the whole, which is what tells you which is which.`],
        [`Write the equation: ${amt} = p \u00b7 ${base}.`,
         `"Is" is the equals sign, "of" is the multiplication. The sentence translates almost word for word once you know that.`],
        [`Divide both sides by ${base}: &nbsp;p = ${fr(amt,base)} = <b>${amt/base}</b>.`,
         `p was multiplied by ${base}, so dividing by ${base} undoes it \u2014 the same one-step equation move from Chapter 3, applied to both sides.`],
        [`That decimal is the percent divided by 100, so multiply by 100: <b>${p}%</b>.`,
         `${amt/base} is the answer expressed per <i>one</i>. A percent wants it per <i>hundred</i>, so scale up by 100 and attach the sign.`]
      ]};
  }
  const orig=pick([40,50,80,120,200,160,250,60]),p=pick([10,15,20,25,30,40]);
  const disc=orig*p/100,fin=orig-disc;
  return{q:`A <b>$${orig}</b> jacket is marked <b>${p}% off</b>. What is the <b>sale price</b>?`,hint:'like 68',ans:'$'+fin,accepts:A(String(fin),'$'+fin,fin.toFixed(2),'$'+fin.toFixed(2)),
    steps:[
      [`The discount is a percent <b>of the original price</b>, so the base is <b>$${orig}</b>.`,
       `Always pin down what the percent is being taken of. Here it's the original price \u2014 stores don't discount off some other number \u2014 and getting the base wrong makes every later step wrong.`],
      [`Find the discount: ${p}% = ${p/100}, and ${p/100} \u00b7 ${orig} = <b>$${disc}</b>.`,
       `Convert to a decimal, then multiply. That's the amount coming <i>off</i>, not the price you pay \u2014 an easy place to stop one step early.`],
      [`Subtract it from the original: $${orig} \u2212 $${disc} = <b>$${fin}</b>.`,
       `The question asked for the sale price, so you owe the subtraction. Answer the question that was asked, not the one you happened to compute.`],
      [`<b>Shortcut worth knowing:</b> ${p}% off means you still pay ${100-p}%, so ${(100-p)/100} \u00b7 ${orig} = <b>$${fin}</b> in one step.`,
       `The whole is 100%. If ${p}% leaves, ${100-p}% stays. Multiplying by ${(100-p)/100} skips the subtraction entirely \u2014 same answer, half the work, and much harder to stop halfway through.`]
    ]};
}});

