function polyStr(cs){
  let out='';
  cs.forEach((c,i)=>{
    const p=cs.length-1-i;if(c===0)return;
    const ac=Math.abs(c);
    const t=p===0?String(ac):((ac===1?'':ac)+'x'+(p>1?'^'+p:''));
    out+= out===''?(c<0?'-'+t:t):(c<0?'-'+t:'+'+t);
  });
  return out||'0';
}
function polyHTML(cs){
  let out='';
  cs.forEach((c,i)=>{
    const p=cs.length-1-i;if(c===0)return;
    const ac=Math.abs(c);
    const t=p===0?String(ac):((ac===1?'':ac)+'x'+(p>1?'<sup>'+p+'</sup>':''));
    out+= out===''?(c<0?'\u2212'+t:t):(c<0?' \u2212 '+t:' + '+t);
  });
  return out||'0';
}
const polyAcc=cs=>A(polyStr(cs),polyStr(cs).replace(/\^/g,''));

T({unit:'Polynomials',short:'Add & subtract polynomials',
title:'Adding and subtracting polynomials',
idea:`<p>A <b>polynomial</b> is a sum of terms, each one a number times a power of x: 3x<sup>2</sup> + 5x \u2212 2. Nothing here is new \u2014 this is <b>combining like terms</b> from Chapter 3, now with more than one power in play.</p>
<p><b>Like terms</b> must match on <b>both</b> the variable and the exponent. 3x<sup>2</sup> and 7x<sup>2</sup> combine. 3x<sup>2</sup> and 7x do not, because x<sup>2</sup> and x are different-sized things \u2014 the same reason ${fr(1,2)} and ${fr(1,3)} couldn't be added until you re-cut them.</p>
<p><b>Adding</b> is just dropping the parentheses and gathering. <b>Subtracting</b> is where people lose points: the minus sign in front of a parenthesis is a <b>\u22121 that must be distributed to every single term inside</b>. Every sign in that second polynomial flips \u2014 not just the first one.</p>`,
watch:`(5x<sup>2</sup> + 3x \u2212 4) \u2212 (2x<sup>2</sup> \u2212 7x + 1) becomes 5x<sup>2</sup> + 3x \u2212 4 \u2212 2x<sup>2</sup> <b>+</b> 7x <b>\u2212</b> 1. All three signs changed. Write the distributed line out before you combine anything \u2014 don't try to flip signs in your head while also adding.`,
gen(){
  const a=[ri(2,9),ri(-8,9),ri(-9,9)],b=[ri(2,9),ri(-8,9),ri(-9,9)];
  const sub=Math.random()<0.5;
  const res=sub?[a[0]-b[0],a[1]-b[1],a[2]-b[2]]:[a[0]+b[0],a[1]+b[1],a[2]+b[2]];
  const neg=b.map(c=>-c);
  return{q:`${sub?'Subtract':'Add'}: &nbsp;<b>(${polyHTML(a)}) ${sub?'\u2212':'+'} (${polyHTML(b)})</b>`,
    hint:'like 3x^2+2x-5',ans:polyHTML(res),accepts:polyAcc(res),
    steps:sub?[
      [`The minus in front of the second parenthesis is really <b>\u22121</b> multiplying everything inside. Distribute it: &nbsp;<b>${polyHTML(neg)}</b>`,
       `Every term flips sign, not just the leading one. ${polyHTML(b)} inside a subtraction means you're removing <i>the whole quantity</i>, so each piece of it gets removed \u2014 including the ones that were already negative, which become positive.`],
      [`Rewrite as one long sum: &nbsp;${polyHTML(a)} ${neg[0]<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(neg[0])}x<sup>2</sup> ${neg[1]<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(neg[1])}x ${neg[2]<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(neg[2])}`,
       `Turning the subtraction into an addition of the opposite is the same move you learned on integers in Chapter 2. One procedure instead of two.`],
      [`Combine the x<sup>2</sup> terms: ${a[0]} ${-b[0]<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b[0])} = <b>${res[0]}</b>.`,
       `Only coefficients combine. You had ${a[0]} of them and removed ${b[0]}, leaving ${res[0]} \u2014 the x<sup>2</sup> is the unit being counted and it never changes.`],
      [`Combine the x terms: <b>${res[1]}</b>, and the constants: <b>${res[2]}</b>.`,
       `Each power gets its own tally. x<sup>2</sup> terms can never merge with x terms, because squares and lengths aren't the same kind of quantity.`],
      [`Write it in descending order: &nbsp;<b>${polyHTML(res)}</b>`,
       `Highest power first is the standard form. It's a convention, but a useful one \u2014 it makes two polynomials instantly comparable.`]
    ]:[
      [`Adding, so the parentheses do nothing \u2014 just drop them: &nbsp;${polyHTML(a)} ${b[0]<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b[0])}x<sup>2</sup> ${b[1]<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b[1])}x ${b[2]<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b[2])}`,
       `A plus sign in front of a parenthesis is a +1 multiplying everything inside, and multiplying by 1 changes nothing. This is exactly why addition is the easy case and subtraction isn't.`],
      [`Combine the x<sup>2</sup> terms: ${a[0]} + (${b[0]}) = <b>${res[0]}</b>.`,
       `Add the coefficients, keep the x<sup>2</sup>. You're counting how many x<sup>2</sup>'s you have in total.`],
      [`Combine the x terms: ${a[1]} + (${b[1]}) = <b>${res[1]}</b>.`,
       `Separate tally, because x and x<sup>2</sup> are unlike terms and can't be pooled together.`],
      [`Combine the constants: ${a[2]} + (${b[2]}) = <b>${res[2]}</b>.`,
       `Third tally. Plain numbers are their own kind of term \u2014 think of them as the x<sup>0</sup> terms.`],
      [`Descending order: &nbsp;<b>${polyHTML(res)}</b>`,
       `Highest power first, standard form. Three separate groups that never merged, because nothing here was like anything else across groups.`]
    ]};
}});

T({unit:'Polynomials',short:'Multiply polynomials',
title:'Multiplying polynomials',
idea:`<p>One rule covers all of it: <b>every term in the first polynomial must multiply every term in the second.</b> That's the distributive property, and it's the same thing you did to 43 \u00d7 27 back in Chapter 1 \u2014 split by place value, multiply each piece by each piece, add the results.</p>
<p>When you multiply the x's, the <b>exponents add</b>: x<sup>2</sup> \u00b7 x<sup>3</sup> = x<sup>5</sup>. Not because of a rule to memorize, but because x<sup>2</sup> is two x's and x<sup>3</sup> is three x's, and lining them all up gives five x's multiplied together. Count the factors and the rule is obvious.</p>
<p>For two binomials, people say <b>FOIL</b> \u2014 First, Outer, Inner, Last. It's a fine memory aid for this one case, but it's just bookkeeping for "everything times everything." FOIL breaks the moment you multiply a binomial by a trinomial; the distributive property never does. Learn the idea, keep FOIL as a shortcut.</p>`,
watch:`(x + 3)<sup>2</sup> is <b>not</b> x<sup>2</sup> + 9. The exponent means (x + 3)(x + 3), and multiplying it out gives x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 9. Squaring a sum is not the same as squaring the pieces \u2014 write out both factors before you multiply anything.`,
gen(){
  if(Math.random()<0.45){
    const k=ri(2,7)*(Math.random()<0.25?-1:1),c=[ri(2,8),ri(-7,8),ri(-8,8)];
    const res=[k*c[0],k*c[1],k*c[2]];
    return{q:`Multiply: &nbsp;<b>${par(k)}(${polyHTML(c)})</b>`,hint:'like 3x^2+2x-5',ans:polyHTML(res),accepts:polyAcc(res),
      steps:[
        [`The ${par(k)} multiplies <b>every</b> term inside \u2014 all three of them.`,
         `${par(k)}(${polyHTML(c)}) means ${Math.abs(k)} cop${Math.abs(k)===1?'y':'ies'} of the whole quantity. Hitting only the first term would be copying part of the thing.`],
        [`${par(k)} \u00b7 ${c[0]}x<sup>2</sup> = <b>${res[0]}x<sup>2</sup></b>`,
         `Multiply the coefficients; the x<sup>2</sup> comes along untouched because ${par(k)} has no x to contribute. ${k<0!==(c[0]<0)?'Different signs, so this term is negative.':'Same signs, so this term is positive.'}`],
        [`${par(k)} \u00b7 ${par(c[1])}x = <b>${res[1]}x</b> &nbsp;and&nbsp; ${par(k)} \u00b7 ${par(c[2])} = <b>${res[2]}</b>`,
         `Carry each term's sign into the multiplication. The polynomial is a <i>sum</i> of signed terms, so ${par(c[1])} and ${par(c[2])} are what you're actually multiplying.`],
        [`Put it together: &nbsp;<b>${polyHTML(res)}</b>`,
         `Three unlike terms, so nothing combines. The powers of x were never changed \u2014 only the coefficients were scaled by ${par(k)}.`]
      ]};
  }
  const a=ri(1,5),b=ri(-8,9),c=ri(1,4),d=ri(-8,9);
  const res=[a*c,a*d+b*c,b*d];
  const t1=a===1?'x':a+'x',t3=c===1?'x':c+'x';
  return{q:`Multiply: &nbsp;<b>(${t1} ${b<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b)})(${t3} ${d<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(d)})</b>`,
    hint:'like 3x^2+2x-5',ans:polyHTML(res),accepts:polyAcc(res),
    steps:[
      [`Every term on the left must meet every term on the right \u2014 four products in all.`,
       `Think of it as ${t1} times the whole second parenthesis, plus ${par(b)} times the whole second parenthesis. That's distributing twice, and it's why exactly four products appear.`],
      [`<b>First:</b> ${t1} \u00b7 ${t3} = <b>${a*c}x<sup>2</sup></b>`,
       `Coefficients multiply (${a} \u00b7 ${c} = ${a*c}) and the x's multiply: x \u00b7 x = x<sup>2</sup>. Two x's multiplied together is x squared \u2014 count the factors and the exponent tells itself.`],
      [`<b>Outer:</b> ${t1} \u00b7 ${par(d)} = ${a*d}x &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Inner:</b> ${par(b)} \u00b7 ${t3} = ${b*c}x`,
       `Both of these come out to plain x terms, because each product has exactly one x in it. That's why the middle term of the answer is a sum of two pieces.`],
      [`<b>Last:</b> ${par(b)} \u00b7 ${par(d)} = <b>${b*d}</b>`,
       `No x anywhere, so this is a constant. ${(b<0)!==(d<0)?'Different signs give a negative.':'Same signs give a positive.'}`],
      [`Combine the two x terms: ${a*d}x ${b*c<0?'\u2212':'+'} ${Math.abs(b*c)}x = <b>${res[1]}x</b>, then write it all in descending order: <b>${polyHTML(res)}</b>`,
       `The Outer and Inner products are like terms \u2014 same variable, same exponent \u2014 so they combine into one. The First and Last terms have nothing to pair with, so they stand alone.`]
    ]};
}});

