T({unit:'Review',short:'Mixed review',mixed:true,
title:'Mixed review',
idea:`<p>Problems pulled at random from every topic in the book, with no label telling you which method to use.</p>
<p>This is the part that actually matters. When you practice one topic at a time, the section heading is quietly doing half the thinking for you \u2014 you already know it's a fraction problem before you read it. On a test, nobody tells you. Being able to <b>identify what kind of problem you're looking at</b> is a separate skill from being able to solve it, and it only gets built by mixing.</p>
<p>Before you compute anything, say out loud what type it is and what the first move should be. If you can name those two things, the rest is machinery you already own.</p>`,
watch:`If you're stuck, don't reach for a formula \u2014 reach for the <b>question</b>. What is being asked? What's the whole and what's the part? What's being done to x? Naming the situation almost always exposes the method.`,
gen(){
  const pool=TOPICS.filter(t=>!t.mixed);
  const t=pick(pool),p=t.gen();
  p.q=`<span class="tag">${t.short}</span> `+p.q;
  return p;
}});


