function sharedPF(a,b){const fa=pfac(a),fb=pfac(b).slice(),out=[];
  fa.forEach(p=>{const i=fb.indexOf(p);if(i>-1){out.push(p);fb.splice(i,1)}});return out}
function allPF(a,b){const fa=pfac(a),tmp=fa.slice(),out=fa.slice();
  pfac(b).forEach(p=>{const i=tmp.indexOf(p);if(i>-1)tmp.splice(i,1);else out.push(p)});
  return out.sort((x,y)=>x-y)}
const listOr=a=>a.length<2?String(a[0]):a.slice(0,-1).join(', ')+' and '+a[a.length-1];

/* ---------- square roots ---------- */
T({unit:'Whole numbers',short:'Square roots',
title:'Square roots and perfect squares',
idea:`<p>Squaring and square-rooting are a <b>question and its answer, running in opposite directions</b>.</p>
<p>Picture an actual square room. If each wall is 7 feet long, the floor area is 7 \u00b7 7 = 49 square feet. That's <b>squaring</b>: you know the wall, you want the floor.</p>
<p>A <b>square root</b> asks the same room the opposite question: <i>the floor is 49 square feet \u2014 how long is one wall?</i> Answer: 7. That's what \u221a49 = 7 means, and it's why the operation is called a "root" \u2014 you're digging back down to the side length that produced the area.</p>
<p>A <b>perfect square</b> is a number whose root is a whole number: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, 121, 144. These are the room sizes that come out to whole feet. Knowing them on sight makes this whole topic fast, so they're worth memorizing the way you memorized times tables.</p>
<p>Everything else lands <b>between</b> two of them. \u221a50 has to be a hair more than 7, because 50 is a hair more than 49. You don't need a calculator to know that \u2014 you need the neighbors.</p>`,
watch:`\u221a25 is <b>5</b>, not 12.5. Rooting is not halving. \u221a100 = 10 and half of 100 is 50 \u2014 wildly different. The root is the number that <i>multiplies by itself</i> to give you the original, so always test your answer by squaring it back.`,
gen(){
  const SQ=[4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81,100,121,144,169,196,225];
  const r=Math.random();
  if(r<0.3){
    const n=pick(SQ),s=Math.round(Math.sqrt(n));
    return{q:`Evaluate: &nbsp;<b>\u221a${n}</b>`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(s),accepts:A(String(s)),
      steps:[
        [`Ask the room question: what number times itself gives <b>${n}</b>?`,
         `Say it that way every time. "\u221a${n}" is shorthand for "the side of a square whose area is ${n}," and phrasing it as a multiplication question turns it into something you can actually search for.`],
        [`Try the perfect squares you know: ${s-1} \u00b7 ${s-1} = ${(s-1)*(s-1)}, and ${s} \u00b7 ${s} = <b>${n}</b>. \u2713`,
         `Walking up through the squares beats guessing. Each one you test also tells you which direction to move next \u2014 too small means go up.`],
        [`So \u221a${n} = <b>${s}</b>.`,
         `Check by squaring back: ${s}<sup>2</sup> = ${n}. Squaring and rooting undo each other, exactly like adding and subtracting, so the check is built into the operation itself.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.55){
    let n;do{n=ri(5,220)}while(SQ.indexOf(n)>-1);
    const lo=Math.floor(Math.sqrt(n)),hi=lo+1;
    return{q:`<b>\u221a${n}</b> falls between two consecutive whole numbers. What is the <b>smaller</b> one?`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(lo),accepts:A(String(lo)),
      steps:[
        [`${n} is not a perfect square, so find the perfect squares on either side of it: <b>${lo*lo}</b> and <b>${hi*hi}</b>.`,
         `Every number sits between two perfect squares, the same way every number sits between two whole numbers on a ruler. Finding the neighbors is the whole technique.`],
        [`${lo*lo} &lt; ${n} &lt; ${hi*hi}, and \u221a${lo*lo} = ${lo}, \u221a${hi*hi} = ${hi}.`,
         `Square roots preserve order: if one area is bigger, its square room has longer walls. So a number squeezed between ${lo*lo} and ${hi*hi} must have a root squeezed between ${lo} and ${hi}.`],
        [`So \u221a${n} is between <b>${lo}</b> and ${hi}. The smaller is <b>${lo}</b>.`,
         `${n-lo*lo < hi*hi-n ? `And you can go further: ${n} is closer to ${lo*lo} than to ${hi*hi}, so \u221a${n} is nearer ${lo} than ${hi}.` : `And ${n} is closer to ${hi*hi}, so \u221a${n} sits nearer ${hi} \u2014 useful when you need a rough decimal.`}`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.8){
    const s=ri(3,15),n=s*s;
    return{q:`Solve for x (positive answer): &nbsp;<b>x<sup>2</sup> = ${n}</b>`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(s),accepts:A(String(s)),
      steps:[
        [`x is being <b>squared</b>. The inverse of squaring is taking the square root.`,
         `Same strategy as every equation you've solved: name what's being done to x, then undo it with the operation built to cancel it.`],
        [`Take the square root of <b>both</b> sides: &nbsp;\u221ax<sup>2</sup> = \u221a${n}`,
         `Both sides, as always \u2014 the balance rule doesn't get suspended just because the operation is new.`],
        [`\u221ax<sup>2</sup> = x, and \u221a${n} = <b>${s}</b> because ${s} \u00b7 ${s} = ${n}.`,
         `The root undoes the square and leaves x standing alone, which was the goal the whole time.`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${s}<sup>2</sup> = ${n} \u2713 &nbsp;<span style="opacity:.7">(\u2212${s} also works, since (\u2212${s})<sup>2</sup> = ${n} \u2014 the problem asked for the positive one.)</span>`,
         `Worth noticing: squaring destroys the sign, so two different numbers can produce the same square. That's why a question like this has to specify which root it wants.`]
      ]};
  }
  const s=ri(4,20),n=s*s;
  return{q:`A square patio covers <b>${n} square feet</b>. How long is each side, in feet?`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(s),accepts:A(String(s),s+' feet',s+'ft'),
    steps:[
      [`Area of a square = side \u00b7 side, so you're told side<sup>2</sup> = <b>${n}</b>.`,
       `This is the literal picture the square root was invented for. The area is what you have; the side is what you want.`],
      [`Undo the squaring with a square root: side = \u221a${n} = <b>${s}</b>.`,
       `${s} \u00b7 ${s} = ${n}, so ${s} feet is the wall length that produces that floor.`],
      [`Each side is <b>${s} feet</b>.`,
       `Watch the units, because they tell the story: the area was in <i>square</i> feet and the answer came out in plain feet. Rooting an area gives back a length \u2014 it strips one of the two dimensions off.`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- GCF & LCM ---------- */
T({unit:'Fractions',short:'GCF & LCM',
title:'Factors, multiples, GCF and LCM',
idea:`<p>These two get confused constantly, so anchor them to pictures.</p>
<p>The <b>greatest common factor</b> is the <b>biggest tile that floors both rooms with no cutting</b>. If one room is 12 ft and the other 18 ft, the largest square tile that fits both perfectly is 6 ft. Factors <i>divide into</i> a number, so the GCF is always <b>smaller than or equal to</b> the numbers you started with.</p>
<p>The <b>least common multiple</b> is the <b>next time two buses leave together</b>. One bus goes every 12 minutes, the other every 18. They both leave at minute 36. Multiples are what you <i>build up to</i>, so the LCM is always <b>bigger than or equal to</b> the numbers.</p>
<p>Prime factorization gives you both. Once you know 12 = 2\u00b72\u00b73 and 18 = 2\u00b73\u00b73, think of each number as a recipe of prime ingredients. The <b>GCF is what both recipes share</b> \u2014 the ingredients you could pull out of either. The <b>LCM is the shopping list that satisfies both recipes</b> \u2014 enough of every ingredient for whichever recipe needs more.</p>
<p>You already use both: GCF to reduce a fraction, LCM to find a common denominator.</p>`,
watch:`If your GCF came out <b>bigger</b> than one of your numbers, or your LCM came out <b>smaller</b>, you've swapped them. Factors shrink, multiples grow \u2014 check the direction before you check the arithmetic.`,
gen(){
  const r=Math.random();
  if(r<0.35){
    const g=ri(2,12),a=g*ri(2,11),b=g*ri(2,11);
    const G=gcd(a,b),sh=sharedPF(a,b);
    return{q:`Find the <b>GCF</b> of <b>${a}</b> and <b>${b}</b>.`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(G),accepts:A(String(G)),
      steps:[
        [`Break each into prime ingredients: ${a} = <b>${pfPlain(a)}</b>, &nbsp;${b} = <b>${pfPlain(b)}</b>.`,
         `Primes are the atoms, and every number has exactly one recipe. Once both recipes are written out, no shared ingredient can hide from you.`],
        [`Circle what appears in <b>both</b> lists: ${sh.length?`<b>${sh.join(' \u00b7 ')}</b>`:'nothing at all'}.`,
         `${sh.length?`Each of these divides into both numbers, so each can be pulled out of either one. Take every shared copy \u2014 ${a} and ${b} both contain ${sh.length>1?'these '+sh.length+' factors':'this factor'}.`:`No prime is in both recipes, which means the two numbers are built from completely different atoms.`}`],
        [`Multiply the shared ingredients: <b>${sh.length?sh.join(' \u00b7 ')+' = '+G:'GCF = 1'}</b>.`,
         `${G===1?`When nothing is shared, the only common factor left is 1. Numbers like this are called relatively prime \u2014 and a fraction made from them is already in lowest terms.`:`${G} is the largest tile that floors both rooms. Anything bigger would need an ingredient one of the recipes doesn't have.`}`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${a} \u00f7 ${G} = ${a/G} and ${b} \u00f7 ${G} = ${b/G} \u2014 both come out whole, and ${a/G} and ${b/G} now share nothing. \u2713`,
         `That second half is the real test. If the leftovers still shared a factor, you'd have found a common factor but not the <i>greatest</i> one.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.7){
    let a,b,g=0;do{a=ri(4,18);b=ri(4,18)}while((a===b||lcm(a,b)>200)&&g++<200);
    const L=lcm(a,b),un=allPF(a,b);
    return{q:`Find the <b>LCM</b> of <b>${a}</b> and <b>${b}</b>.`,hint:'a whole number',ans:String(L),accepts:A(String(L)),
      steps:[
        [`Write both recipes: ${a} = <b>${pfPlain(a)}</b>, &nbsp;${b} = <b>${pfPlain(b)}</b>.`,
         `Same starting move as the GCF. The difference is what you do with the two lists afterward.`],
        [`Build a shopping list with enough of <b>every</b> ingredient for whichever recipe needs more: <b>${un.join(' \u00b7 ')}</b>.`,
         `The LCM has to be divisible by both numbers, so it must contain everything ${a} needs <i>and</i> everything ${b} needs. Where they overlap you don't buy twice \u2014 you just take the larger amount.`],
        [`Multiply it out: <b>${un.join(' \u00b7 ')} = ${L}</b>.`,
         `${L===a*b?`Here ${a} and ${b} share no ingredients, so the shopping list is simply everything from both \u2014 which is why the LCM came out to ${a} \u00b7 ${b}.`:`Notice ${L} is less than ${a} \u00b7 ${b} = ${a*b}, because the shared ingredients only had to be bought once.`}`],
        [`<b>Check:</b> ${L} \u00f7 ${a} = ${L/a} and ${L} \u00f7 ${b} = ${L/b} \u2014 both whole, so ${L} really is a multiple of each. \u2713`,
         `And it's the <i>least</i> one: drop any ingredient from that list and it stops being divisible by one of them.`]
      ]};
  }
  let a,b,g=0;do{a=ri(4,15);b=ri(4,15)}while((a===b||lcm(a,b)>120)&&g++<200);
  const L=lcm(a,b);
  return{q:`Two buses leave the depot together. One returns every <b>${a} minutes</b>, the other every <b>${b} minutes</b>. In how many minutes will they next leave <b>together</b>?`,
    hint:'a whole number',ans:String(L),accepts:A(String(L)),
    steps:[
      [`List when each one leaves. Bus A: ${[1,2,3,4].map(k=>a*k).join(', ')}, \u2026 &nbsp;Bus B: ${[1,2,3,4].map(k=>b*k).join(', ')}, \u2026`,
       `Each bus's departure times are just the multiples of its interval. Writing a few out makes the question concrete: you're hunting for a time that shows up on both lists.`],
      [`You need a time on <b>both</b> lists \u2014 a common multiple \u2014 and the <b>first</b> one, so the <b>least</b> common multiple.`,
       `"Next time together" is exactly what LCM means. This is why the word "least" is in the name: there are infinitely many times they coincide, and you want the earliest.`],
      [`${a} = ${pfPlain(a)}, ${b} = ${pfPlain(b)} &rarr; LCM = ${allPF(a,b).join(' \u00b7 ')} = <b>${L}</b>.`,
       `The prime method beats listing once the numbers get big \u2014 the lists would run for pages, but the recipes stay short.`],
      [`They next leave together in <b>${L} minutes</b>.`,
       `Check it: ${L} \u00f7 ${a} = ${L/a} trips for the first bus, ${L} \u00f7 ${b} = ${L/b} for the second. Both whole numbers, so both are genuinely back at the depot. \u2713`]
    ]};
}});

/* ---------- comparing & ordering ---------- */
T({unit:'Fractions',short:'Comparing numbers',
title:'Comparing and ordering fractions and decimals',
idea:`<p>You can't compare prices in dollars and yen without converting first. Fractions are the same: <b>${fr(3,5)} versus ${fr(5,8)} is a comparison in two different currencies</b>, and until you convert, the numbers on the page tell you nothing.</p>
<p>Three honest ways to convert, all giving the same verdict:</p>
<p><b>Common denominator.</b> Re-cut both into the same size pieces, then just count. ${fr(3,5)} = ${fr(24,40)} and ${fr(5,8)} = ${fr(25,40)}, so the second is bigger by one fortieth.</p>
<p><b>Cross multiply.</b> Compare 3 \u00b7 8 = 24 against 5 \u00b7 5 = 25. This is the common-denominator method with the bookkeeping hidden \u2014 you're comparing the numerators you <i>would</i> have gotten, without writing the denominator down.</p>
<p><b>Turn both into decimals.</b> 0.6 versus 0.625. Decimals are pre-converted to a common currency \u2014 tenths, hundredths, thousandths \u2014 which is exactly why they're easy to compare.</p>
<p>For decimals, <b>compare place by place from the left</b>, like alphabetizing. The first seat where they differ decides it, and nothing further right can overturn it.</p>`,
watch:`More digits does not mean bigger. 0.7 &gt; 0.65, because the tenths seat decides before the hundredths ever gets a vote. Pad with zeros \u2014 0.70 versus 0.65 \u2014 and the comparison becomes obvious.`,
gen(){
  const r=Math.random();
  if(r<0.4){
    let a,b,c,d,g=0;
    do{b=ri(3,12);d=ri(3,12);a=ri(1,b-1);c=ri(1,d-1)}while((a*d===b*c||b===d)&&g++<200);
    const big=a*d>c*b?[a,b]:[c,d],L=lcm(b,d);
    return{q:`Which is <b>greater</b>: &nbsp;<b>${fr(a,b)}</b> or <b>${fr(c,d)}</b>?`,hint:'like 3/4',
      ans:fr(big[0],big[1]),accepts:A(fracForms(big[0],big[1]),big[0]+'/'+big[1]),
      steps:[
        [`Different denominators means different currencies \u2014 the pieces aren't the same size, so ${a} and ${c} can't be compared as they stand.`,
         `A bigger numerator proves nothing on its own. ${fr(1,2)} beats ${fr(3,8)} even though 1 &lt; 3, because halves are much bigger pieces than eighths.`],
        [`Re-cut both to ${L}ths: &nbsp;${fr(a,b)} = <b>${fr(a*(L/b),L)}</b> &nbsp;and&nbsp; ${fr(c,d)} = <b>${fr(c*(L/d),L)}</b>.`,
         `${L} is the least common multiple of ${b} and ${d}. Multiplying each fraction by a form of 1 changes only its appearance, so both amounts are untouched \u2014 now they're just written in the same currency.`],
        [`Same size pieces, so count them: ${a*(L/b)} versus ${c*(L/d)} &rarr; <b>${fr(big[0],big[1])}</b> is greater.`,
         `Once the denominators match, the numerators are a straight count and the bigger count wins.`],
        [`<b>Shortcut \u2014 cross multiply:</b> ${a} \u00b7 ${d} = ${a*d} against ${c} \u00b7 ${b} = ${c*b}. Bigger cross product sits above the bigger fraction.`,
         `This isn't a separate rule. Multiplying ${fr(a,b)} by ${fr(d,d)} and ${fr(c,d)} by ${fr(b,b)} would give both a denominator of ${b*d} \u2014 and those cross products are exactly the numerators you'd get. The shortcut just skips writing the shared denominator, since it can't affect which is larger.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.7){
    const [n,d]=pick([[3,5],[5,8],[2,3],[3,4],[7,10],[5,6],[1,4],[4,5],[7,8],[3,8]]);
    const fv=n/d;
    let dv;do{dv=Math.round((fv+ (Math.random()<0.5?1:-1)*(ri(2,14)/100))*100)/100}while(dv<=0||dv===fv);
    const fBig=fv>dv;
    return{q:`Which is <b>greater</b>: &nbsp;<b>${fr(n,d)}</b> or <b>${dv}</b>?`,hint:'fraction or decimal',
      ans:fBig?fr(n,d):String(dv),accepts:fBig?A(fracForms(n,d)):A(decForms(String(dv))),
      steps:[
        [`One is a fraction and one is a decimal \u2014 two notations, so convert to a single one first.`,
         `Comparing them side by side as written is like comparing a price tag in euros to one in dollars. Pick a currency before you judge.`],
        [`Turn the fraction into a decimal: the bar means divide, so ${n} \u00f7 ${d} = <b>${fv}</b>.`,
         `Decimals are the easier target here, because they're already expressed in tenths and hundredths \u2014 a shared currency by construction.`],
        [`Line them up by place value: <b>${fv.toFixed(3)}</b> versus <b>${dv.toFixed(3)}</b>.`,
         `Pad with zeros so both have the same number of seats. Zeros on the end of a decimal add nothing to its value; they just stop your eye from comparing tenths against hundredths.`],
        [`Read left to right \u2014 the first seat where they differ settles it &rarr; <b>${fBig?fr(n,d)+' ('+fv+')':dv}</b> is greater.`,
         `Place value decides in order of importance. A difference in the tenths seat is worth ten times a difference in the hundredths, so once tenths disagree, nothing behind them can catch up.`]
      ]};
  }
  const den=pick([12,24,20,16]);
  let ns=[];while(ns.length<3){const v=ri(1,den-1);if(ns.indexOf(v)<0)ns.push(v)}
  const items=ns.map(v=>{const [sn,sd]=simp(v,den);return{v,sn,sd}});
  const sorted=items.slice().sort((x,y)=>x.v-y.v);
  const disp=items.map(o=>fr(o.sn,o.sd)).join(', ');
  const ansStr=sorted.map(o=>fr(o.sn,o.sd)).join(', ');
  const plain=sorted.map(o=>o.sn+'/'+o.sd);
  return{q:`Put in order from <b>least to greatest</b>: &nbsp;<b>${disp}</b>`,hint:'like 1/4, 3/8, 1/2',
    ans:ansStr,accepts:A(plain.join(', '),plain.join(','),plain.join(' ')),
    steps:[
      [`Give them all the same denominator so they're in one currency. Every one of these can be written in <b>${den}ths</b>: &nbsp;${items.map(o=>fr(o.v,den)).join(', ')}.`,
       `They came from ${den}ths originally, which is why ${den} works for all three. Re-cutting each into ${den}ths is multiplying by a form of 1 \u2014 the amounts don't move, only their labels.`],
      [`Now the pieces are identical in size, so just read the counts: ${items.map(o=>o.v).join(', ')}.`,
       `This is the payoff of a common denominator. Comparing three fractions became comparing three whole numbers, which needs no thought at all.`],
      [`Sort the counts smallest to largest: ${sorted.map(o=>o.v).join(' &lt; ')}.`,
       `More pieces of the same size means more stuff. Ordering the numerators orders the fractions, guaranteed, as long as the denominators match.`],
      [`Translate back to the original forms: &nbsp;<b>${ansStr}</b>`,
       `Answer in the notation the question used. The ${den}ths were scaffolding \u2014 useful for deciding the order, then taken down.`]
    ]};
}});

