T({unit:'Geometry & data',short:'Perimeter & area',
title:'Perimeter, area and volume',
idea:`<p>Three measurements, three completely different jobs. Keep the pictures straight and you'll never mix up the formulas.</p>
<p><b>Perimeter</b> is the <b>fence around the yard</b>. You walk the edge and add up how far you went. It's a length, so it's measured in plain feet.</p>
<p><b>Area</b> is the <b>carpet covering the floor</b>. You're filling a flat surface, so you count squares \u2014 hence <b>square</b> feet. For a rectangle it's length \u00b7 width, because you're laying down rows: ${8} squares per row, ${5} rows, 40 squares.</p>
<p><b>Volume</b> is the <b>water that fills the box</b>. Now you're filling space in three directions, so you count cubes \u2014 <b>cubic</b> feet.</p>
<p>A triangle's area is <b>half</b> the rectangle it fits inside: ${fr(1,2)} \u00b7 base \u00b7 height. Draw any triangle, box it in, and you'll see the leftover corners are exactly the same size as the triangle. That's the whole reason for the ${fr(1,2)} \u2014 it isn't a magic constant, it's one of two matching halves.</p>`,
watch:`The units tell you whether you used the right formula. Perimeter comes out in feet, area in square feet, volume in cubic feet. If a problem asks how much fencing you need and your answer is in square feet, you computed the carpet instead of the fence.`,
gen(){
  const r=Math.random();
  if(r<0.28){
    const L=ri(4,25),W=ri(3,22);
    return{q:`A rectangle is <b>${L} ft</b> long and <b>${W} ft</b> wide. Find its <b>perimeter</b>.`,hint:'a number, in ft',ans:String(2*L+2*W),accepts:A(String(2*L+2*W),(2*L+2*W)+'ft',(2*L+2*W)+' feet'),
      steps:[
        [`Perimeter is the fence \u2014 walk all the way around and add every side.`,
         `Start from the picture, not the formula. If you can see yourself walking the edge, you'll never accidentally multiply.`],
        [`A rectangle has <b>two</b> lengths and <b>two</b> widths: ${L} + ${W} + ${L} + ${W}.`,
         `Opposite sides of a rectangle are equal, which is what makes it a rectangle. So you only need two measurements to know all four sides.`],
        [`Add them: <b>${2*L+2*W} ft</b>. &nbsp;<span style="opacity:.7">(Same thing as 2(${L}) + 2(${W}), or 2(${L} + ${W}).)</span>`,
         `All three forms are the same arithmetic \u2014 the last one is just the distributive property factoring out the 2. Use whichever you'll remember under pressure.`],
        [`Units: <b>feet</b>, not square feet.`,
         `You added lengths, so you got a length. Nothing here counted squares, and the units are your receipt for what you actually did.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.5){
    const L=ri(4,20),W=ri(3,18);
    return{q:`A rectangle is <b>${L} ft</b> long and <b>${W} ft</b> wide. Find its <b>area</b>.`,hint:'a number, in sq ft',ans:String(L*W),accepts:A(String(L*W),(L*W)+'sqft',(L*W)+' square feet'),
      steps:[
        [`Area is the carpet \u2014 you're covering the floor with 1 ft \u00d7 1 ft squares and counting them.`,
         `Holding the picture keeps area and perimeter from blurring together. Carpet fills; fence surrounds.`],
        [`One row across the length holds <b>${L}</b> squares, and there are <b>${W}</b> rows going back.`,
         `This is where the formula comes from. Area isn't length \u00b7 width because someone declared it \u2014 it's ${L} per row, ${W} rows, and multiplication is repeated addition of equal rows.`],
        [`${L} \u00b7 ${W} = <b>${L*W} square feet</b>.`,
         `Every one of those ${L*W} squares is one square foot, so the count and the area are the same number.`],
        [`Units: <b>square feet</b>, because you counted squares.`,
         `Two lengths multiplied give a squared unit: ft \u00b7 ft = ft<sup>2</sup>. The units multiply right alongside the numbers.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.75){
    const b=ri(3,20)*2,h=ri(3,18);
    return{q:`A triangle has base <b>${b} cm</b> and height <b>${h} cm</b>. Find its <b>area</b>.`,hint:'a number, in sq cm',ans:String(b*h/2),accepts:A(String(b*h/2),(b*h/2)+'sqcm'),
      steps:[
        [`Box the triangle inside a rectangle ${b} cm by ${h} cm.`,
         `Any triangle fits exactly inside a rectangle with the same base and height. Drawing that box is what makes the formula obvious instead of memorized.`],
        [`That rectangle's area is ${b} \u00b7 ${h} = <b>${b*h}</b> square cm.`,
         `Length \u00b7 width, same as before. This is the amount of carpet the full box would need.`],
        [`The triangle is exactly <b>half</b> of it: ${fr(1,2)} \u00b7 ${b*h} = <b>${b*h/2} square cm</b>.`,
         `Cut the rectangle along the triangle's slanted side and the leftover pieces reassemble into a triangle identical to the first. Two matching halves, so the triangle gets ${fr(1,2)}.`],
        [`Units: <b>square cm</b>.`,
         `Still counting squares, so still a squared unit. Taking half of an area doesn't change what kind of measurement it is.`]
      ]};
  }
  const l=ri(2,12),w=ri(2,10),h=ri(2,9);
  return{q:`A box is <b>${l} in</b> long, <b>${w} in</b> wide and <b>${h} in</b> tall. Find its <b>volume</b>.`,hint:'a number, in cubic in',ans:String(l*w*h),accepts:A(String(l*w*h),(l*w*h)+'cubicinches'),
    steps:[
      [`Volume is the water that fills the box \u2014 or, more usefully, the number of 1 in \u00d7 1 in \u00d7 1 in cubes that pack inside it.`,
       `Counting cubes is the picture that makes the formula self-explanatory, the same way counting squares did for area.`],
      [`The bottom layer is a rectangle ${l} by ${w}, so it holds ${l} \u00b7 ${w} = <b>${l*w}</b> cubes.`,
