}});

T({unit:'Geometry & data',short:'Mean, median & mode',
title:'Mean, median, mode and range',
idea:`<p>Four ways to describe a pile of numbers, each answering a different question.</p>
<p><b>Mean</b> (the average) is the <b>fair split</b>. Everyone throws their money into one pot and it gets divided evenly \u2014 that's why you add everything and divide by how many there are. It's what each person <i>would</i> have if the pile were shared out equally.</p>
<p><b>Median</b> is the <b>middle person in a line-up</b>. Sort everyone by height and walk to the middle of the row. With an even number there's no single middle person, so you average the two standing there.</p>
<p><b>Mode</b> is the <b>most popular choice</b> \u2014 the value that shows up most often. Think of the shoe size a store sells the most of. Mode is the only one of the four that works on things that aren't numbers.</p>
<p><b>Range</b> is the <b>spread</b>: biggest minus smallest. It measures how far apart the extremes are, not where the middle is.</p>
<p>Why have both mean and median? Because <b>one billionaire in a room wrecks the mean but barely moves the median.</b> The fair-split average gets dragged by extreme values; the middle person doesn't care how rich the person at the end of the line is.</p>`,
watch:`<b>Sort the list first</b> when finding the median. The middle of the unsorted list is meaningless \u2014 it's the middle of the line-up you want, and there's no line-up until you've ordered everyone.`,
gen(){
  const r=Math.random();
  const nums=k=>Array.from({length:k},()=>ri(2,40));
  if(r<0.3){
    const k=ri(4,6);let v;do{v=nums(k)}while(v.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)%k!==0);
    const s=v.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0),m=s/k;
    return{q:`Find the <b>mean</b> of: &nbsp;<b>${v.join(', ')}</b>`,hint:'a number',ans:String(m),accepts:A(String(m)),
      steps:[
        [`Mean is the fair split, so first put everything in one pot: ${v.join(' + ')} = <b>${s}</b>.`,
         `Adding is you collecting every contribution into a single pile. Nothing is lost \u2014 the total is exactly what was there before, just gathered.`],
        [`Count how many there are to share among: <b>${k}</b> values.`,
         `This count is the divisor, and it's the step people skip. Divide by ${k} because ${k} values contributed, not by anything you see in the list.`],
        [`Split the pot evenly: ${s} \u00f7 ${k} = <b>${m}</b>.`,
         `That's what each one would be if they all had to be equal. Notice it lands inside the range of the data \u2014 an average can never be bigger than the biggest or smaller than the smallest.`],
        [`<b>Sanity check:</b> ${m} \u00b7 ${k} = ${s}, the original total. \u2713`,
         `Multiplying back has to rebuild the pot. If it doesn't, you either mis-added or divided by the wrong count.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.6){
    const k=pick([5,6,7]);const v=nums(k),s=v.slice().sort((a,b)=>a-b);
    const mid=k%2?s[(k-1)/2]:(s[k/2-1]+s[k/2])/2;
    return{q:`Find the <b>median</b> of: &nbsp;<b>${v.join(', ')}</b>`,hint:'a number',ans:String(mid),accepts:A(String(mid)),
      steps:[
        [`Line them up smallest to largest: <b>${s.join(', ')}</b>.`,
         `The median is the middle of the <i>line-up</i>, so there's nothing to find until the line-up exists. Sorting isn't preparation, it's half the work.`],
        k%2?[`There are ${k} values \u2014 an odd count \u2014 so exactly one stands in the middle: position ${(k+1)/2}.`,
          `With an odd count there are the same number of people on each side, so one person is genuinely in the center. Count in from both ends and you'll meet at them.`]
         :[`There are ${k} values \u2014 an even count \u2014 so <b>two</b> share the middle: ${s[k/2-1]} and ${s[k/2]}.`,
          `An even line-up has no single center person; the middle falls in the gap between two of them. So you take the point halfway between.`],
        k%2?[`The median is <b>${mid}</b>.`,
          `Half the values sit at or below it and half at or above. That's the definition of the middle, and it's why one wildly large value at the end of the list wouldn't budge it.`]
         :[`Average the two middles: (${s[k/2-1]} + ${s[k/2]}) \u00f7 2 = <b>${mid}</b>.`,
          `You're finding the exact midpoint of the gap. The median doesn't have to be one of the original values \u2014 it just has to split the group in half.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.8){
    const base=nums(4),rep=pick(base);const v=base.concat([rep,rep]).sort(()=>Math.random()-0.5);
    return{q:`Find the <b>mode</b> of: &nbsp;<b>${v.join(', ')}</b>`,hint:'a number',ans:String(rep),accepts:A(String(rep)),
      steps:[
        [`Mode means most popular, so tally how many times each value appears.`,
         `You're not doing arithmetic here \u2014 you're counting repeats. Mode is the only one of these measures that never adds or divides anything.`],
        [`Sorting makes duplicates sit together: <b>${v.slice().sort((a,b)=>a-b).join(', ')}</b>.`,
         `Repeats are easy to miss in a scrambled list and impossible to miss in a sorted one. Same trick that helped with the median.`],
        [`<b>${rep}</b> appears ${v.filter(x=>x===rep).length} times \u2014 more than anything else. That's the mode.`,
         `Think of it as the best-selling size on the shelf. It says what's <i>typical</i> in the sense of most common, which can be very different from the average.`]
      ]};
  }
  const k=5,v=nums(k),s=v.slice().sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  return{q:`Find the <b>range</b> of: &nbsp;<b>${v.join(', ')}</b>`,hint:'a number',ans:String(s[k-1]-s[0]),accepts:A(String(s[k-1]-s[0])),
    steps:[
      [`Range measures spread, so find the two extremes: largest is <b>${s[k-1]}</b>, smallest is <b>${s[0]}</b>.`,
       `Only the ends matter. Everything in between could be rearranged completely and the range wouldn't change \u2014 that's both its strength and its weakness.`],
      [`Subtract: ${s[k-1]} \u2212 ${s[0]} = <b>${s[k-1]-s[0]}</b>.`,
       `Subtraction measures distance between two points, which is precisely what "how spread out" is asking.`],
      [`The range is <b>${s[k-1]-s[0]}</b>.`,
       `It tells you the width of the data, not its center. Two groups can share an identical mean and have completely different ranges \u2014 one tightly bunched, one scattered.`]
    ]};
}});

T({unit:'Geometry & data',short:'Coordinate plane',
title:'The coordinate plane',
idea:`<p>The coordinate plane is a <b>street grid</b>. Two number lines cross at zero: the <b>x-axis</b> running east\u2013west, the <b>y-axis</b> running north\u2013south. Their crossing point is the <b>origin</b>, (0, 0).</p>
<p>A point is an address, written <b>(x, y)</b> \u2014 always in that order. The memory hook: <b>you walk down the hallway before you climb the stairs.</b> Go across first, then up or down. (3, 5) and (5, 3) are two different corners of town.</p>
<p>Signs are directions, exactly as on the number line. Positive x is right, negative x is left. Positive y is up, negative y is down.</p>
<p>The two axes chop the plane into four <b>quadrants</b>, numbered <b>counterclockwise starting from the top right</b>: I is (+, +), II is (\u2212, +), III is (\u2212, \u2212), IV is (+, \u2212). You don't need to memorize the list \u2014 read the signs and picture where that puts you.</p>`,
watch:`(0, 4) and (\u22123, 0) are in <b>no quadrant</b>. A point sitting on an axis is on the border between two of them, like standing in the middle of the street rather than on one side.`,
gen(){
  const R=['I','II','III','IV'];
  const r=Math.random();
  if(r<0.4){
    const x=ri(1,9)*(Math.random()<0.5?-1:1),y=ri(1,9)*(Math.random()<0.5?-1:1);
    const q0=x>0?(y>0?0:3):(y>0?1:2);
    return{q:`In which <b>quadrant</b> does the point <b>(${sgn(x)}, ${sgn(y)})</b> lie?`,hint:'I, II, III or IV',
      ans:R[q0],accepts:A(R[q0],String(q0+1),'quadrant '+R[q0],'quadrant '+(q0+1)),
      steps:[
        [`Read the address in order: x = <b>${sgn(x)}</b>, y = <b>${sgn(y)}</b>.`,
         `Hallway first, then stairs. The first number is always the east\u2013west move and the second is always north\u2013south \u2014 swapping them lands you somewhere else entirely.`],
        [`x is ${x>0?'positive, so walk <b>right</b>':'negative, so walk <b>left</b>'} from the origin.`,
         `The x-axis is a number line laid flat, so its signs mean the same thing they always did: positive to the right of zero, negative to the left.`],
        [`y is ${y>0?'positive, so go <b>up</b>':'negative, so go <b>down</b>'}.`,
         `The y-axis is the same number line stood upright. Positive is above zero, negative below.`],
        [`${x>0?'Right':'Left'} and ${y>0?'up':'down'} puts you in the ${x>0?(y>0?'top-right':'bottom-right'):(y>0?'top-left':'bottom-left')} corner \u2014 <b>Quadrant ${R[q0]}</b>.`,
         `Quadrants are numbered counterclockwise from the top right, so the ${x>0?(y>0?'top-right corner is first':'bottom-right is the last one, IV'):(y>0?'top-left is second':'bottom-left is third')}. Picture the corner and the number follows \u2014 no list to memorize.`]
      ]};
  }
  if(r<0.72){
    const x=ri(-8,8),y=ri(-8,8);
    const dx=ri(1,7)*(Math.random()<0.5?-1:1),dy=ri(1,7)*(Math.random()<0.5?-1:1);
    const nx=x+dx,ny=y+dy;
    return{q:`Start at <b>(${sgn(x)}, ${sgn(y)})</b>. Move <b>${Math.abs(dx)} unit${Math.abs(dx)===1?'':'s'} ${dx>0?'right':'left'}</b> and <b>${Math.abs(dy)} unit${Math.abs(dy)===1?'':'s'} ${dy>0?'up':'down'}</b>. Where do you land?`,
      hint:'like (3,-2)',ans:`(${sgn(nx)}, ${sgn(ny)})`,accepts:A(`(${nx},${ny})`,`${nx},${ny}`,`(${nx} ${ny})`),
      steps:[
        [`Moving ${dx>0?'right':'left'} changes only the <b>x</b>: ${sgn(x)} ${dx>0?'+':'\u2212'} ${Math.abs(dx)} = <b>${sgn(nx)}</b>.`,
         `East\u2013west movement is pure x. Your y can't change while you're walking down the hallway \u2014 the two directions are independent, which is the entire point of using two axes.`],
        [`Moving ${dy>0?'up':'down'} changes only the <b>y</b>: ${sgn(y)} ${dy>0?'+':'\u2212'} ${Math.abs(dy)} = <b>${sgn(ny)}</b>.`,
         `Same idea on the stairs. Note that moving ${dy>0?'up adds':'down subtracts'} \u2014 identical to travelling ${dy>0?'right':'left'} on a plain number line, just stood on end.`],
        [`New address: <b>(${sgn(nx)}, ${sgn(ny)})</b>.`,
         `Write x first out of habit, every time. The order is the only thing making an ordered pair meaningful.`]
      ]};
  }
  const vertical=Math.random()<0.5;
  const a=ri(-8,8),b=ri(-8,8);
  let c=ri(-8,8);while(c===b)c=ri(-8,8);
  const p1=vertical?[a,b]:[b,a],p2=vertical?[a,c]:[c,a];
  const dist=Math.abs(b-c);
  return{q:`How far apart are <b>(${sgn(p1[0])}, ${sgn(p1[1])})</b> and <b>(${sgn(p2[0])}, ${sgn(p2[1])})</b>?`,
    hint:'a whole number',ans:String(dist),accepts:A(String(dist)),
    steps:[
      [`Compare the addresses: the <b>${vertical?'x':'y'}</b>-values are both ${vertical?sgn(a):sgn(a)}, so these two points sit on the same ${vertical?'vertical':'horizontal'} line.`,
       `Spotting the matching coordinate is what makes this easy. When one coordinate is shared, the points differ in only one direction and the distance is a straight walk \u2014 no diagonal.`],
      [`Only the <b>${vertical?'y':'x'}</b>-values differ: ${sgn(b)} and ${sgn(c)}.`,
       `So the whole distance is the gap between those two numbers on a single number line. The other coordinate contributes nothing because it never changed.`],
      [`Distance is the gap, and gaps are never negative: |${sgn(b)} \u2212 ${sgn(c)}| = <b>${dist}</b> units.`,
       `Take the absolute value because distance has no direction. Whether you walk from ${sgn(b)} to ${sgn(c)} or the other way, it's ${dist} units of walking either way.`]
    ]};
}});

